const TOPICS = [
  // ─────────────────── STATE MACHINE FRAMEWORK ──────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m16", "lld_m16_t5", 5, "Design a State Machine Framework", "design-state-machine-framework",
    ["case-study", "state-machine", "framework", "declarative"],
    "An order moves CREATED → PAID → PACKED → SHIPPED → DELIVERED, with CANCELLED reachable from three of those, RETURNED only after delivery, and a refund action that itself fires another event. Six months later the payments team adds PARTIALLY_PAID and the ops team adds ON_HOLD from anywhere. Your teammate has written a 900-line switch. Design a State Machine FRAMEWORK that any team can configure instead.",
    "The entire difficulty collapses once transitions stop being CONTROL FLOW and become DATA: a table keyed by (state, event) yielding a target state, an optional guard, and an action. A switch encodes the machine in the shape of the code, so every question — 'what can happen from PACKED?', 'is DELIVERED reachable from CREATED?', 'draw me the diagram' — requires reading all 900 lines. A table answers all three with a query. The rest of the design is firing ORDER (exit → transition action → entry, exactly once, in that order), a deliberate policy for unmatched events, hierarchical states to kill duplicated edges, and an event QUEUE so an action that fires another event does not re-enter a half-applied transition.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: a reusable framework — not one hardcoded machine — that lets a team DECLARE a finite state machine and then run instances of it. Declaration must express: a finite set of STATES with one initial state and zero or more terminal states; a set of EVENTS; and TRANSITIONS of the form (sourceState, event) → targetState, each optionally carrying a GUARD (a boolean predicate over the event payload and the machine's context that must hold for the transition to be taken) and an ACTION (a side effect run as part of taking it). States may declare ENTRY and EXIT actions that run whenever the machine enters or leaves them, regardless of which transition caused it. At runtime the API is small: fire(event) returns whether a transition occurred, currentState() reports where the machine is, and the machine must expose enough structure to be introspected — 'which events are accepted here?', 'is this state reachable?' — and rendered as a diagram. Additional functional asks that interviewers layer on: HIERARCHICAL (nested) states so common transitions are declared once on a parent; transition listeners for auditing every state change; and persistence, so a long-lived machine (an order living for weeks) can be rehydrated from a stored state name. Non-functional constraints that actually shape the class model: the machine definition is declared ONCE and shared across thousands of instances, so definition must be immutable and instance state must be tiny; validation of the definition (unknown target states, duplicate (state,event) keys, unreachable states) must happen at BUILD time, not on the unlucky production event; transitions must be ATOMIC from an observer's perspective — no observer may see a half-applied transition; and the machine must be safe when an action fires another event re-entrantly, which is common (an entry action for PAID immediately firing AUTO_SHIP). Explicitly out of scope: distributed coordination and durable workflow retries — those are workflow-engine concerns; this is the in-process kernel they would sit on."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "STATE is an identity plus two optional hooks: an id (an enum or a string), onEntry(context, event), and onExit(context, event). It deliberately does NOT know its outgoing transitions — putting them there is what recreates the switch, one method per state. In a hierarchical machine a State also carries an optional parent reference, which is the only structural change hierarchy needs. EVENT is a typed message: a name plus an optional payload; guards and actions read it but never mutate the machine. TRANSITION is the framework's central value object: source, event name, target, an optional Guard, and an optional Action. It is immutable and self-describing, which is what makes the whole machine printable. GUARD is a functional interface — boolean test(Context ctx, Event e) — kept strictly SIDE-EFFECT FREE, because a guard may be evaluated and then rejected when another transition is chosen or the event is ignored. ACTION is the side-effecting counterpart — void execute(Context ctx, Event e). TRANSITIONTABLE is the storage and lookup structure: a Map<StateId, Map<EventName, List<Transition>>>, a list because several transitions may share a (state, event) key and be disambiguated by guards in declaration order. STATEMACHINEDEFINITION is the immutable, validated whole: states, the table, the initial state, and the validation results; it is built by a fluent BUILDER and then shared. STATEMACHINE is the per-instance runtime: a reference to the definition, the current state id, a Context (the domain object the machine drives — the Order itself), an EVENTQUEUE for re-entrant fires, and a processing flag. LISTENERS observe transitions for audit and metrics; they are notified after the transition completes, never inside it."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The load-bearing decision is DECLARATIVE TABLE over imperative dispatch, and you should argue it in terms of what each representation makes cheap. In the switch design, the machine's structure is implicit in the code's control flow: to answer 'what transitions leave PACKED?' a human must read a case block and every nested if inside it. In the table design the machine is a data structure, so structural questions become lookups: accepted events at a state is a keySet(); reachability is a graph traversal; a Graphviz/PlantUML render is a loop over the table; validation (dangling targets, duplicate keys, states with no outgoing edges, states unreachable from initial) is a build-time pass. That single property — the machine is inspectable — is worth more than the code it saves. The patterns, each justified rather than named: BUILDER for definition construction, because a definition must be validated as a whole and then frozen; the fluent form (from(CREATED).on(PAY).to(PAID).guard(...).action(...)) makes the declaration read like the state chart, which matters because the declaration IS the documentation. STRATEGY (as small functional interfaces) for Guard and Action, so behaviour is injected rather than switched on. OBSERVER for transition listeners, decoupling audit/metrics from the kernel. FLYWEIGHT in effect: one immutable definition shared by every instance — an order-processing service with 200,000 live orders holds 200,000 tiny StateMachine objects and exactly one definition. What is deliberately NOT used: the classic GoF STATE pattern (one class per state, each implementing handle()) — it is the right answer when each state has rich, divergent BEHAVIOUR beyond transitions, but here it scatters the transition graph across N classes and reintroduces the very problem the table solves; say that explicitly, because interviewers expect the GoF reflex and reward the candidate who knows when it is the wrong tool. Finally, keep the framework generic over the context type so the same kernel drives orders, subscriptions, and TCP connections without casting."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "final class Transition<S, E, C> {\n  final S source, target; final E event;\n  final BiPredicate<C, Object> guard;      // null = always true; MUST be pure\n  final BiConsumer<C, Object> action;      // null = no side effect\n  Transition(S s, E e, S t, BiPredicate<C, Object> g, BiConsumer<C, Object> a) {\n    source = s; event = e; target = t; guard = g; action = a;\n  }\n}\n\nfinal class Definition<S, E, C> {\n  final S initial;\n  final Map<S, Map<E, List<Transition<S, E, C>>>> table;   // (state,event) -> ordered candidates\n  final Map<S, BiConsumer<C, Object>> onEntry, onExit;\n  final Map<S, S> parent;                                   // hierarchical states\n  Definition(S initial, Map<S, Map<E, List<Transition<S, E, C>>>> t,\n             Map<S, BiConsumer<C, Object>> en, Map<S, BiConsumer<C, Object>> ex, Map<S, S> par) {\n    this.initial = initial; this.table = t; this.onEntry = en; this.onExit = ex; this.parent = par;\n  }\n  /** Walk up the parent chain: a child inherits every transition its ancestors declare. */\n  Transition<S, E, C> resolve(S state, E event, C ctx, Object payload) {\n    for (S s = state; s != null; s = parent.get(s)) {\n      for (Transition<S, E, C> t : table.getOrDefault(s, Map.of()).getOrDefault(event, List.of()))\n        if (t.guard == null || t.guard.test(ctx, payload)) return t;   // first guard that passes\n    }\n    return null;                                              // no transition matches\n  }\n}\n\nclass StateMachine<S, E, C> {\n  private final Definition<S, E, C> def;\n  private final C context;\n  private S current;\n  private final Deque<Object[]> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();   // re-entrant fires\n  private boolean processing = false;\n  private final boolean strict;            // unmatched-event policy: throw vs ignore\n  private final List<TransitionListener<S, E>> listeners = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();\n\n  StateMachine(Definition<S, E, C> d, C ctx, boolean strict) {\n    def = d; context = ctx; current = d.initial; this.strict = strict;\n  }\n\n  public synchronized boolean fire(E event, Object payload) {\n    queue.addLast(new Object[]{ event, payload });\n    if (processing) return true;          // an action fired us: ENQUEUE, never recurse\n    processing = true;\n    try {\n      boolean any = false;\n      while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n        Object[] q = queue.pollFirst();\n        any |= step((E) q[0], q[1]);\n      }\n      return any;\n    } finally { processing = false; }\n  }\n\n  private boolean step(E event, Object payload) {\n    Transition<S, E, C> t = def.resolve(current, event, context, payload);\n    if (t == null) return onUnhandled(event);          // deliberate policy, see below\n    S from = current;\n    run(def.onExit.get(from), payload);                // 1. exit the old state\n    if (t.action != null) t.action.accept(context, payload);   // 2. transition action\n    current = t.target;                                 // 3. state changes here\n    run(def.onEntry.get(t.target), payload);            // 4. enter the new state\n    listeners.forEach(l -> l.onTransition(from, event, t.target));\n    return true;\n  }\n\n  private void run(BiConsumer<C, Object> hook, Object payload) {\n    if (hook != null) hook.accept(context, payload);\n  }\n\n  /** Policy, chosen once and documented: IGNORE is right for noisy event streams,\n   *  THROW for command APIs where a bad transition is a caller bug. */\n  private boolean onUnhandled(E event) {\n    if (strict) throw new IllegalStateException(\"no transition: \" + current + \" on \" + event);\n    return false;\n  }\n\n  public S state() { return current; }\n  public Set<E> acceptedEvents() { return def.table.getOrDefault(current, Map.of()).keySet(); }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the transition table as data — why a switch is the wrong representation",
        "Start with what everyone writes first, because the interviewer wants you to demolish it precisely. Version one is a method handle(Event e) with a switch on the current state; inside each case, another switch or an if-ladder on the event; inside those, the guard conditions; inside those, the mutation and the side effect. It works. It also has five properties that make it unmaintainable, and naming them is the answer. (1) The machine is not INSPECTABLE: 'which events does PACKED accept?' cannot be answered by any program, only by a human reading code — so the state chart in the wiki drifts from the code within one sprint, and the drift is invisible until an incident. (2) It cannot be VALIDATED: a typo that transitions to a state nobody handles compiles fine and fails on one production order at 2am; with a table, the builder rejects an unknown target before the process is healthy enough to serve traffic. (3) It cannot be COMPOSED: adding ON_HOLD from any of eleven states means editing eleven case blocks, and forgetting one is silent. (4) It cannot be CONFIGURED: every machine change is a code change, a review, and a deploy — whereas a table can be loaded from JSON/YAML, letting an ops team add a state without a release (with the honest caveat that new guard/action TYPES are still code; only wiring is data). (5) It cannot be TESTED structurally: you can only test behaviours you thought to enumerate; with a table you can assert graph properties — every state reachable from initial, every non-terminal state has an outgoing edge, no state has two unguarded transitions on the same event (nondeterminism). Now the representation itself: Map<State, Map<Event, List<Transition>>>. The nesting gives O(1) lookup of the candidate list for the current (state, event). It is a LIST, not a single Transition, because guarded transitions legitimately share a key: PAID on SHIP goes to SHIPPED when the address is verified and to ON_HOLD when it is not. Candidates are tried in DECLARATION ORDER and the first passing guard wins — a deterministic rule that must be documented, because the alternative (evaluate all, error if more than one matches) is also defensible and detects contradictory rules at the cost of always paying for every guard. Since guards may be evaluated and discarded, guards MUST be pure; a guard that mutates or that charges a card is a bug the framework cannot protect you from, so state the contract loudly. The payoff of all this is the moment the interviewer says 'add PARTIALLY_PAID': in the table design it is one builder line plus one state declaration, and the validator tells you immediately if you left it unreachable."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: entry/exit actions, firing order, and the unmatched-event policy",
        "Three actions can fire on one transition — the source state's onExit, the transition's own action, and the target state's onEntry — and the ORDER is a specification, not an implementation detail. The correct order is exit → transition action → state assignment → entry, and each part earns its position. onExit runs FIRST and while current is still the source state, because its job is to tear down what the source set up (stop the 'awaiting payment' timer, release a lock, close a session) and it must be able to see the state it is leaving. The transition ACTION runs next: it is the work specific to THIS edge, and it is the correct place for effects that belong to neither state — 'charge the card when PENDING moves to PAID via CONFIRM' should not live on PAID's entry, because PAID may also be entered from a manual reconciliation edge that must not charge again. The state assignment happens after the action so that a failing action leaves the machine in a consistent, un-advanced state (whether an exception should abort the transition or leave it half-applied is a policy you must state — aborting before assignment is the sane default). onEntry runs LAST, after current is the target, so anything it does — including firing another event or reading state() — sees the new state. Two subtleties worth volunteering. INTERNAL vs EXTERNAL transitions: a self-transition PAID → PAID normally counts as leaving and re-entering, so exit and entry both fire (restarting that timer, which is often exactly what a 'retry' means); when you want the action WITHOUT the entry/exit churn, the framework needs an explicit internal-transition flag — most real frameworks have one because 'increment a counter without resetting the timeout' is a common need. Hierarchy interacts here: moving between two children of the same parent must NOT fire the parent's exit and entry; a correct implementation computes the least common ancestor of source and target and fires exits up to it, then entries down from it, which is the same LCA walk a UML statechart engine performs. THE UNMATCHED EVENT is a genuine design decision, not an oversight. Three policies, each right somewhere: IGNORE (return false and carry on) suits noisy external event streams where a duplicate PAYMENT_CONFIRMED webhook is normal and must be a harmless no-op; THROW suits command APIs where fire(CANCEL) on a DELIVERED order is a caller bug that must not be swallowed; QUEUE/DEFER — hold the event until a state that accepts it is entered — is what UML calls a deferred event and is powerful but memory-unbounded without a cap. Whatever you pick, make it a configurable definition-level policy with a default, log it with the (state, event) pair, and count it as a metric: a spike in unmatched events is the earliest signal that reality has grown a transition your table lacks."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: hierarchy, re-entrant events, and thread safety",
        "HIERARCHICAL STATES exist to kill duplicated edges. Concretely: an order can be CANCELLED from CREATED, PAID, and PACKED but not from SHIPPED or DELIVERED. Flat, that is three identical transitions, and when a fourth pre-shipment state arrives someone forgets the fourth edge. Nest the three under an ACTIVE_PRESHIPMENT parent, declare CANCEL once on the parent, and every present and future child inherits it — the resolve() loop in the code walks the parent chain and takes the first matching, guard-passing transition, so a child can also OVERRIDE the parent's edge by declaring its own (most-specific wins, exactly like method overriding, and stating that rule is what makes the design predictable). Hierarchy also gives shared entry/exit semantics — the parent's onEntry runs when entering any child from outside the parent, and not when moving between siblings — which is where the LCA walk from the previous section pays off. The cost is real and worth conceding: resolution is now O(depth) instead of O(1), the diagram is harder to read, and 'where does this transition actually come from?' needs tooling. Use hierarchy when you have genuinely shared edges, not to look sophisticated. RE-ENTRANT EVENTS are the bug that makes naive frameworks unsafe. An entry action for PAID fires AUTO_SHIP. If fire() simply recurses, the inner transition runs while the outer transition is mid-flight: depending on where you put the state assignment, the inner step reads a stale current, or the outer's listener notification reports a transition to a state the machine has already left, or two exits fire for one entry. Worse, an action that fires an event that loops back re-enters until the stack overflows. The fix is the RUN-TO-COMPLETION semantics UML mandates and the code implements: every fire() appends to a queue; if a fire arrives while processing is true, it returns immediately after enqueuing, and the outer loop drains the queue after the current transition has fully completed. The machine is therefore always in a well-defined state at the moment any event begins processing, listeners always see completed transitions, and infinite loops become a drainable queue you can bound and detect (cap iterations per fire and throw with the event trace — an event ping-pong is a modelling error you want surfaced, not a hang). THREAD SAFETY: the definition is immutable and shared freely; the instance is mutable and must be guarded. For a per-entity machine the simplest correct answer is a lock on the instance — synchronized on fire(), as coded — because contention is naturally sharded by entity and the critical section is short. Note what that lock does NOT do: it does not protect the CONTEXT if other code mutates the order concurrently, and it does not make actions safe to run inside the lock — a transition action doing I/O (charging a card, calling a shipping API) while holding the machine lock is a latency bomb and can deadlock if that call re-enters the machine from another thread. The disciplined answer is to keep actions fast and in-memory, publish side effects to an outbox/queue drained outside the lock, and — when the machine is persisted — make the state change and the outbox write one transaction so a crash between them cannot lose the effect."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "TABLE vs GoF STATE CLASSES vs SWITCH: the table wins when the interesting content is the transition GRAPH (workflows, orders, protocols) — it is inspectable, validatable, and configurable. One-class-per-state wins when each state has substantial divergent BEHAVIOUR beyond routing (a TCP connection whose states implement genuinely different send/receive logic), because there the polymorphism carries real code, not just edges. The switch wins for a machine with three states that will never grow — and admitting that a 3-state machine does not need a framework is senior signal, not weakness. CODE-DEFINED vs DATA-DEFINED DEFINITIONS: a fluent builder gives compile-time checking of state and event enums and IDE navigation; loading YAML/JSON lets non-engineers add states and lets one deployment serve tenant-specific workflows, at the cost of runtime-only validation and a registry mapping guard/action names to implementations. Most teams start with the builder and add a loader when a second tenant appears. GUARD SEMANTICS: first-match-wins is fast and needs an ordering rule; evaluate-all-and-reject-ambiguity catches contradictory rules at the cost of evaluating every guard on every event — pick by whether your risk is latency or modelling errors. PERSISTENCE: storing just the current state id is cheap and makes rehydration trivial, but it loses history and cannot answer 'how long were we in PACKED?'; storing an append-only transition LOG (event sourcing in miniature) gives audit, replay, and debugging for the price of a write per event — for orders, payments, and anything regulated, take the log. WHAT BREAKS AT SCALE: nothing about the kernel — instances are tiny and the definition is shared — but the moment transitions must survive process restarts, involve timeouts ('cancel if unpaid for 30 minutes'), or coordinate across services, you are building a WORKFLOW ENGINE: timers become scheduled events from a durable store, actions need idempotency keys because retries will re-run them, and long-running machines need versioning so an in-flight order created under definition v1 is not resumed under v3 with a state that no longer exists — pin the definition version on the instance. Extensions worth naming: transition listeners for audit and metrics; a Graphviz exporter generated straight from the table (documentation that cannot drift); a model-checking pass for reachability and deadlock states; and orthogonal/parallel regions when an entity genuinely has two independent state dimensions (payment status and fulfilment status), which is usually better modelled as two machines than one product-state explosion."),
    ],
    "State machine frameworks are a favourite senior LLD prompt because the naive answer — a switch — is not wrong, it is unmaintainable, and the interview is entirely about whether you can articulate why and design the alternative. The graded moves are: representing transitions as DATA in a (state, event) table so the machine can be validated, introspected, and rendered; specifying the exact firing order of exit, transition action, and entry, and knowing that self-transitions and hierarchical moves change it; choosing a deliberate policy for unmatched events instead of falling into one; using nested states to eliminate duplicated edges; and handling re-entrant fires with run-to-completion queuing rather than recursion. Candidates who reflexively reach for the GoF State pattern and cannot say when it is the wrong tool, or who never notice that an action firing an event breaks a recursive implementation, are the ones this question is designed to separate.",
    [
      G("switch_based_machine", "Encoding the machine as a switch on state with nested if-ladders on events, so the transition graph exists only as control flow.", "Store transitions as data — Map<State, Map<Event, List<Transition>>> with guard and action per edge — so the definition can be validated at build time, queried, rendered as a diagram, and extended without editing shared methods.", "handle(event) with a case per state; adding a state requires touching every case block; no way to answer 'which events does this state accept?' programmatically; a wiki diagram that no longer matches the code."),
      G("unspecified_action_order", "Leaving the order of exit action, transition action, and entry action implicit — or running entry before the state is actually assigned.", "Specify and implement exit → transition action → state assignment → entry; keep edge-specific effects on the transition, state lifecycle effects on entry/exit, and compute the least common ancestor for hierarchical moves so a sibling transition does not re-fire the parent's hooks.", "onEntry observing the OLD state via state(); a self-transition silently skipping exit/entry with no internal-transition flag; effects that must run once running twice because they sit on both an entry hook and a transition."),
      G("recursive_reentrant_fire", "Letting an action or entry hook call fire() recursively, so a nested transition runs while the outer one is half-applied.", "Adopt run-to-completion: enqueue every fire and drain the queue after the current transition finishes; return immediately when already processing, bound the drain to detect event ping-pong, and notify listeners only on completed transitions.", "StackOverflowError from looping events; listeners reporting transitions out of order; current state read inside an action disagreeing with the transition that is executing; no queue or processing flag anywhere in fire()."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.statemachine, "State machine framework class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── BUILD SYSTEM / TASK RUNNER ───────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m16", "lld_m16_t6", 6, "Design a Build System / Task Runner", "design-build-system",
    ["case-study", "graph", "concurrency", "caching"],
    "A repo has 400 build tasks. A developer changes one comment in one file and the build takes eleven minutes, recompiling everything. Another developer touches nothing and gets a DIFFERENT binary because a checkout reset an mtime. A third runs with -j8 and gets a corrupted artifact once a week. Design a build system that skips what has not changed, parallelises what is independent, and is deterministic enough that those three bugs cannot happen.",
    "Everything rests on one contract: each TASK declares its INPUTS and OUTPUTS. Given that, three properties fall out that are otherwise impossible. The dependency DAG can be topologically ordered and cycle-checked, so execution order is derived rather than hand-maintained. Up-to-date checks become a CONTENT FINGERPRINT — a hash of input contents plus the task's own recipe — which is why unchanged work is skipped correctly where timestamps lie. And independent tasks can be run in parallel SOUNDLY, because declared outputs are what prove two tasks cannot write the same file. Undeclared inputs are the original sin of build systems: they silently break incremental correctness and parallel safety at the same time.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: a user declares TASKS — a name, a set of DEPENDENCIES on other tasks, a set of INPUT files or directories, a set of OUTPUT paths, and an ACTION that produces the outputs from the inputs (compile, bundle, run tests, generate code). The runner is invoked with one or more requested targets and must execute exactly the transitive closure of their dependencies, in an order where every task runs after everything it depends on. It must support INCREMENTAL builds: a task whose inputs, recipe, and dependencies' outputs are unchanged since its last successful run is SKIPPED, and the build reports it as up-to-date. It must support PARALLEL execution of independent tasks with a user-supplied degree of parallelism. It must handle FAILURE sanely: a failed task fails its dependents transitively, unrelated branches may still complete (a --keep-going mode) or the build may abort fast, and the exit status plus a per-task report says which tasks ran, were skipped, or failed. Nice-to-haves interviewers reach for: a clean/dry-run mode, a shared remote cache so a task another machine already ran is downloaded rather than recomputed, and file watching for continuous rebuilds. Non-functional requirements carry the weight. CORRECTNESS OF SKIPPING is the whole product: a false skip ships a stale artifact and destroys trust in the tool permanently, which is why the up-to-date check must not depend on clocks. DETERMINISM: the same inputs must produce the same result regardless of the parallelism level or task ordering, so parallel and serial builds cannot disagree. CYCLE DETECTION must be a build-time error with the cycle printed, not a hang or a stack overflow. And the graph is large (thousands of nodes), so analysis must be linear-ish — O(V+E) — and the hashing work must be cacheable, since re-hashing every file in a large repo on every invocation is itself a performance problem worth designing around."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "TASK is the unit of work and the contract: an id, a list of dependency ids, declared INPUTS (file paths or globs), declared OUTPUTS (file paths), and an action to execute. It is a pure declaration — it never decides whether it should run, because that decision needs global information the task does not have. Insisting that inputs and outputs are DECLARED, not discovered by watching what the action happens to touch, is the design's central demand and everything else depends on it. TASKGRAPH holds the tasks and their edges, and owns the two graph operations: a topological ORDER over the requested closure and CYCLE DETECTION (the same DFS, reporting the actual back edge so the error message names the loop). FINGERPRINT is the value object that answers 'has anything relevant changed?': a strong hash (SHA-256) over the sorted list of input file content hashes, the task's recipe identity (command line, action version, environment variables it declares as relevant), and the fingerprints of its dependencies — a Merkle chain, so a change deep in the graph invalidates everything downstream without any explicit propagation code. FILEHASHER computes and memoises per-file content hashes within a build, keyed by path, with an optional (path, size, mtime) → hash cache across builds so a large unchanged tree is not re-read every time — note this uses mtime as an optimisation HINT only, never as the correctness signal. BUILDCACHE maps fingerprint → recorded outputs (locally: 'this fingerprint succeeded, outputs are in place'; remotely: the output blobs themselves), enabling both skipping and cross-machine reuse. SCHEDULER owns execution: a READY SET of tasks whose dependencies have all completed, a bounded worker pool, and the in-degree bookkeeping that moves tasks into the ready set as their dependencies finish. TASKRESULT records OK / SKIPPED / FAILED with timing and the fingerprint, and is what failure propagation and the final report read. BUILDENGINE is the facade: resolve targets, validate the graph, then hand the ready set to the scheduler."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The execution model is deliberately not 'topologically sort, then run the list' — that is correct but serial. Instead, keep the topological structure implicit in an IN-DEGREE COUNTER per task (Kahn's algorithm, run incrementally): every task starts with a count of unfinished dependencies; tasks at zero enter the ready set; when a task completes, each dependent's counter is decremented and any that hits zero becomes ready. This is the same algorithm that produces a topological order, but expressed so that the ready set is naturally the maximal set of tasks that may run CONCURRENTLY at this moment — the sequential topological sort is just the degenerate single-worker case. Cycle detection falls out for free: if the run finishes with tasks still holding a positive in-degree, those tasks form a cycle, and a DFS over them prints it. Patterns, justified rather than listed: COMMAND for the task action (an executable unit with a uniform interface, queueable and retryable); a small STRATEGY seam for the up-to-date check, because 'content hash' vs 'timestamp' vs 'always run' are genuinely swappable policies and having the seam lets you prove the timestamp mode is wrong by running both; MEMOIZATION/PROXY around the file hasher; and OBSERVER for build listeners so progress reporting, timing, and logging do not tangle into the scheduler. The dependency direction matters for reuse: Task knows nothing about the scheduler, the cache, or fingerprints — it is a declaration, which is why the same task objects can be executed, dry-run, printed as a graph, or shipped to a remote executor. Two decisions to defend explicitly. First, the up-to-date check is computed by the ENGINE, not the task: it needs the dependency fingerprints, so only the graph owner can compute it. Second, the fingerprint includes the dependencies' fingerprints rather than their output timestamps, which is what makes invalidation transitive and cheap. What is deliberately absent: a global build lock (it would serialize the very parallelism the design exists for — the ceiling is per-task isolation enforced by declared outputs), and any attempt to infer inputs by tracing syscalls, which is a real technique (Bazel's sandboxing, ptrace-based systems) but a different, heavier design worth naming as the escape hatch when declarations cannot be trusted."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "final class Task {\n  final String id;\n  final List<String> deps;\n  final List<Path> inputs, outputs;      // DECLARED — the whole design rests on this\n  final String recipe;                   // command line / action identity, part of the hash\n  final Runnable action;\n  Task(String id, List<String> deps, List<Path> in, List<Path> out, String recipe, Runnable a) {\n    this.id = id; this.deps = deps; this.inputs = in; this.outputs = out;\n    this.recipe = recipe; this.action = a;\n  }\n}\n\nclass Fingerprinter {\n  private final Map<Path, String> fileHashes = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n  String hashFile(Path p) {\n    return fileHashes.computeIfAbsent(p, path -> sha256(Files.readAllBytes(path)));  // CONTENT, not mtime\n  }\n  /** Merkle-style: a dependency's fingerprint is folded in, so deep changes invalidate downstream. */\n  String fingerprint(Task t, Map<String, String> depFingerprints) {\n    MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance(\"SHA-256\");\n    md.update(t.recipe.getBytes(UTF_8));\n    t.inputs.stream().sorted().forEach(p -> md.update(hashFile(p).getBytes(UTF_8)));\n    t.deps.stream().sorted().forEach(d -> md.update(depFingerprints.get(d).getBytes(UTF_8)));\n    return HexFormat.of().formatHex(md.digest());\n  }\n}\n\nclass BuildEngine {\n  private final Map<String, Task> tasks;\n  private final Fingerprinter fp = new Fingerprinter();\n  private final BuildCache cache;\n  private final Map<String, String> fingerprints = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n  private final Map<String, TaskResult> results = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n  BuildEngine(Map<String, Task> tasks, BuildCache c) { this.tasks = tasks; this.cache = c; }\n\n  BuildReport run(List<String> targets, int parallelism) throws InterruptedException {\n    Set<String> closure = transitiveClosure(targets);\n    Map<String, Integer> inDegree = new HashMap<>();\n    Map<String, List<String>> dependents = new HashMap<>();\n    for (String id : closure) {\n      inDegree.put(id, (int) tasks.get(id).deps.stream().filter(closure::contains).count());\n      for (String d : tasks.get(id).deps) dependents.computeIfAbsent(d, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(id);\n    }\n    ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(parallelism);   // BOUNDED\n    Deque<String> ready = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    inDegree.forEach((id, d) -> { if (d == 0) ready.add(id); });\n    int done = 0, inFlight = 0;\n    CompletionService<TaskResult> cs = new ExecutorCompletionService<>(pool);\n    while (done < closure.size()) {\n      while (!ready.isEmpty()) { String id = ready.poll(); cs.submit(() -> execute(tasks.get(id))); inFlight++; }\n      if (inFlight == 0) break;                       // nothing runnable and not finished => CYCLE\n      TaskResult r = cs.take().get(); inFlight--; done++;\n      results.put(r.id, r);\n      for (String dep : dependents.getOrDefault(r.id, List.of())) {\n        if (r.status == Status.FAILED) { results.put(dep, TaskResult.skippedDueToFailure(dep)); done++; continue; }\n        if (inDegree.merge(dep, -1, Integer::sum) == 0) ready.add(dep);\n      }\n    }\n    pool.shutdown();\n    if (done < closure.size()) throw new IllegalStateException(\"dependency cycle: \" + findCycle(closure, inDegree));\n    return new BuildReport(results);\n  }\n\n  private TaskResult execute(Task t) {\n    Map<String, String> depFps = new HashMap<>();\n    for (String d : t.deps) depFps.put(d, fingerprints.get(d));\n    String key = fp.fingerprint(t, depFps);\n    fingerprints.put(t.id, key);\n    if (cache.isUpToDate(key, t.outputs)) return TaskResult.skipped(t.id, key);   // incremental win\n    t.action.run();\n    cache.record(key, t.outputs);\n    return TaskResult.ok(t.id, key);\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the task DAG — topological order, incremental readiness, and cycle detection",
        "Dependencies form a DIRECTED ACYCLIC GRAPH and every property of the runner derives from that. Order is not authored; it is COMPUTED, which is precisely the improvement over a shell script where a human maintains the order and gets it subtly wrong when a new dependency appears. Two standard algorithms produce it, and the choice is not cosmetic. DFS-based topological sort emits nodes in reverse post-order and detects cycles by colouring — white/grey/black, where an edge into a grey node is a back edge and therefore a cycle, and the grey stack at that moment IS the cycle to print. It is elegant and gives the best error message, but it produces a fixed linear order, which is the wrong shape for a parallel runner. KAHN'S ALGORITHM — maintain an in-degree per node, repeatedly take nodes with in-degree zero, decrement their dependents — produces the same orders but exposes something extra: at every step, the set of zero-in-degree nodes is exactly the set of tasks that are legally runnable RIGHT NOW, all of them, simultaneously. That is why the engine above keeps in-degrees instead of a sorted list; the parallel scheduler is not bolted onto a topological sort, it IS the topological sort with more than one node taken at a time. Complexity for both is O(V+E), and the closure computation is a straightforward reverse-reachability from the requested targets — important because a user asking for one target must not pay for the other 380 tasks in the repo. CYCLE DETECTION deserves to be treated as a first-class feature, not an assertion. If a cycle exists, Kahn's loop terminates with tasks remaining and in-degree greater than zero for all of them; the engine then runs a DFS restricted to those nodes to recover an actual cycle and reports 'a → b → c → a'. Compare the alternative: a naive recursive builder with no detection recurses until a StackOverflowError, or worse, deadlocks silently while workers wait on each other. The message matters as much as the detection — a build tool that says 'cycle detected' without naming the edges makes a 4,000-node graph unsearchable. Two subtleties worth volunteering. First, a build with --keep-going must continue running branches unrelated to a failure, which means failure propagation is a graph operation too: mark the failed task's transitive dependents as SKIPPED_DUE_TO_FAILURE and continue draining the rest of the ready set, rather than aborting the pool. Second, the graph must be validated before any action runs: a dependency on an undefined task, or a cycle, should fail in milliseconds, not after seven minutes of successful compilation."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: incremental builds — content fingerprints, and exactly why mtime lies",
        "The naive up-to-date rule is Make's: run the task if any input's modification time is newer than any output's. It is cheap and it is wrong in several routine, expensive ways, and reciting them concretely is what separates a candidate who has used build systems from one who has suffered them. (1) CLOCK GRANULARITY AND SKEW: some filesystems store mtimes at one-second resolution, so a file written and consumed within the same second appears not-newer and the task is falsely skipped; on network filesystems and CI runners with drifting clocks, an input can be stamped in the past relative to an output, producing the same false skip — the worst possible failure, a silently stale artifact. (2) VCS CHECKOUTS AND RESTORES: git checkout, cache restores, and rsync set mtime to NOW for files whose content did not change, so switching branches back and forth triggers full rebuilds of identical content — the false-rebuild direction, which is merely expensive but destroys the incremental promise. (3) REVERTING A CHANGE: edit a file, build, then undo the edit and build again. Content is byte-identical to two builds ago, but mtime is newer, so everything downstream rebuilds; with content hashing the fingerprint matches a cached entry and the whole subtree is skipped instantly. (4) NON-CONTENT CHANGES: touch changes mtime with no content change at all. The correct signal is CONTENT. Compute a strong hash of each declared input's bytes, sort the input list for determinism, and fold in the task's own RECIPE — the command line, the compiler version, the relevant environment variables — because changing a compiler flag must invalidate every output even though no source file changed; this is the step that people forget and it produces the classic 'clean build differs from incremental build' bug. Then fold in the DEPENDENCIES' FINGERPRINTS rather than their output files, which turns the whole thing into a Merkle tree: change one header, its fingerprint changes, every transitive dependent's fingerprint changes automatically, and no propagation code exists to have a bug in. The resulting key is the cache key, and that single fact is what makes a REMOTE cache possible: if any machine has ever executed this exact fingerprint, its outputs can be downloaded instead of computed, so a developer's first build after pulling can be nearly instant. Two honest costs. Hashing is I/O: reading every input on every invocation is slower than stat-ing it, which is why real systems keep a (path, size, mtime) → hash side cache — mtime as a HINT to skip re-reading, never as the correctness decision, so a stale hint costs a re-hash rather than a wrong answer. And reproducibility must extend to the actions themselves: if a compiler embeds a timestamp or a random build id in its output, identical inputs yield different outputs and the cache cannot dedupe downstream — which is why hermetic, deterministic actions are a prerequisite for aggressive caching, not an aesthetic preference."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: safe parallel execution and failure propagation",
        "PARALLELISM IS ONLY SOUND BECAUSE OF DECLARATIONS. Two tasks with no path between them in the DAG may run concurrently — that is the theory. In practice they may still collide if either touches a file it did not declare: task A writes build/tmp/gen.h as an undeclared side effect while task B reads it, and the result depends on scheduling, which is exactly the 'corrupted artifact once a week under -j8' symptom in the prompt. Undeclared inputs and outputs break BOTH properties at once — they make incremental skipping wrong (the input that changed was not hashed) and parallelism unsafe (the write that raced was not known) — which is why 'tasks must declare inputs and outputs' is the single most important sentence in this design, and why systems that take correctness seriously go further and ENFORCE it: run each action in a sandbox where only declared inputs are visible and only declared outputs can be written, so an undeclared dependency fails loudly and immediately instead of racing. Name that as the escape hatch when declarations cannot be trusted, along with its cost (sandbox setup per action, which is significant). THE POOL MUST BE BOUNDED. Spawning a thread or process per ready task is a classic failure: a wide graph puts 400 compilers on an 8-core machine, each with a large working set, and the build thrashes or is OOM-killed — a fixed pool sized to cores (with memory-per-action considered, since linkers can need gigabytes) makes throughput predictable. The scheduler pattern in the code — submit the ready set, block on a CompletionService for the next finisher, decrement dependents' in-degrees, submit anything newly ready — keeps every worker busy without ever running a task before its dependencies. Its liveness argument is worth stating: the loop only blocks when at least one task is in flight, and a task in flight always eventually completes or fails, so the only way to stall is a cycle, which the loop detects by finding nothing runnable with work remaining. WORK SHAPE matters too: a long pole task (a slow link step) with many dependents limits total speedup regardless of core count — Amdahl in build form — so the critical path length, not the task count, is the number to optimise, and reporting it is a genuinely useful feature. FAILURE PROPAGATION: when a task fails, its transitive dependents cannot possibly succeed and must be marked SKIPPED_DUE_TO_FAILURE rather than attempted; in fail-fast mode the pool is shut down and in-flight tasks are interrupted, while in --keep-going mode independent branches continue so the developer sees every error in one run instead of one per iteration. Two production details that earn credit: a failed action must not leave PARTIAL OUTPUTS that a later invocation mistakes for valid — write to a temporary path and atomically rename on success, and never record a cache entry for a failed run; and the cache must be written only after outputs are complete, so a crash mid-action cannot poison the cache with a fingerprint whose outputs do not exist."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "TIMESTAMPS vs CONTENT HASHES: timestamps are near-free (a stat per file) and are why Make is fast to start; content hashes cost I/O proportional to the input set but are the only correct signal across checkouts, restores, reverts, and multiple machines — and they are the prerequisite for a shared cache. The pragmatic middle, used by nearly every modern system, is content hashes with an mtime-and-size side cache to avoid re-reading unchanged files: correctness from content, speed from the hint. TASK-LEVEL vs FILE-LEVEL GRANULARITY: coarse tasks ('compile the module') are simple to declare but rebuild everything in the module for a one-file change; fine tasks (one per source file) maximise skipping but multiply graph size and per-task overhead — for fast actions the fingerprinting can cost more than the work, so granularity is a tuning decision, not a principle. DECLARED vs DISCOVERED DEPENDENCIES: declarations are simple and fast but drift from reality (a new #include that nobody declared); discovery via compiler dependency output (gcc -MD) or syscall tracing is accurate but ties the build system to toolchain specifics and slows every action — the common answer is declarations plus a toolchain-emitted dependency file folded into the fingerprint. LOCAL vs REMOTE CACHE: a remote cache turns a colleague's build into your download and is transformative on CI, but it demands hermetic actions (no absolute paths, no embedded timestamps, pinned toolchains) and raises a trust question — a poisoned cache entry ships a compromised binary everywhere, so entries should be keyed by full fingerprint and, in security-sensitive settings, be signed or restricted to trusted writers. THREADS vs PROCESSES for the pool: processes give isolation and let sandboxing work but cost startup; threads are cheap but let one action's leak or crash take down the runner. WHAT BREAKS AT SCALE: the fingerprinting itself becomes the bottleneck on a repo with hundreds of thousands of files, which pushes toward a persistent daemon holding warm hashes and a file-watcher (inotify/FSEvents) that invalidates only what changed — Gradle's daemon and Bazel's server exist for exactly this. Extensions worth naming: dynamic dependencies discovered during execution (a task that generates other tasks) which force the graph to be mutable mid-build and complicate the in-degree bookkeeping; a --dry-run/--explain mode that prints WHY each task will run (which input's hash changed) and is the single best debugging feature such a system can have; and per-task resource declarations so memory-hungry link steps are not scheduled eight at a time."),
    ],
    "Build systems are a strong senior LLD prompt because they force three distinct competencies into one design: graph algorithms (topological order via in-degrees, cycle detection with a printable cycle), caching and invalidation correctness (content fingerprints with the recipe and dependency fingerprints folded in, and a concrete account of why mtime is unsound), and bounded concurrency (a ready-set scheduler whose soundness rests entirely on declared inputs and outputs). Interviewers probe the seams: 'why not timestamps?', 'I changed a compiler flag — does it rebuild?', 'two tasks write the same temp file, what happens?', 'there's a cycle — what does the user see?'. Candidates who treat the up-to-date check as trivial, or who describe parallelism without noticing that it requires declarations to be sound, get found out immediately; candidates who can name the Merkle-chained fingerprint as the thing that also enables a remote cache are visibly operating at the level the question is aimed at.",
    [
      G("mtime_up_to_date", "Deciding a task is up to date by comparing input and output modification times.", "Fingerprint CONTENT: hash the declared inputs, fold in the recipe/command line and the dependencies' fingerprints, and compare against the last successful run; keep an mtime+size cache only as a hint to skip re-hashing unchanged files.", "if (input.lastModified() > output.lastModified()) rebuild; full rebuilds after a branch switch that changed nothing; a stale artifact after a same-second write; a compiler-flag change that does not trigger any rebuild."),
      G("undeclared_io_parallel", "Running independent tasks in parallel while tasks read or write files they never declared.", "Make declared inputs/outputs the contract that authorises parallelism — and enforce it with sandboxing when declarations cannot be trusted; write outputs to a temp path and rename atomically so a failed action leaves nothing partial.", "intermittent corrupt artifacts only at high -j values; a task that works serially and fails in parallel; shared scratch directories not listed in any task's outputs; cache entries recorded for actions that later failed."),
      G("no_cycle_detection", "Executing dependencies by naive recursion with no cycle check and no failure propagation.", "Track in-degrees (Kahn) so the ready set is computed; if work remains with nothing runnable, run a DFS over the remainder and report the actual cycle; mark a failed task's transitive dependents SKIPPED_DUE_TO_FAILURE instead of attempting them.", "StackOverflowError or a hang on a circular dependency; an error that says 'cycle detected' without naming the edges; dependents of a failed task still being executed; graph validation happening only after the first actions have run."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.buildsystem, "Build system class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── MAZE GENERATOR & SOLVER ──────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m16", "lld_m16_t7", 7, "Design a Maze Generator & Solver", "design-maze",
    ["case-study", "strategy", "graph", "algorithms"],
    "Generate a 60×40 maze with exactly one path between any two cells, then find the shortest route from the top-left to the bottom-right. Your first attempt draws walls that exist on one side of a boundary but not the other, your second generates something with a visible bias — every corridor snakes forever — and your DFS solver returns a path 900 cells long when a 140-cell path exists. Design the maze generator and solver properly.",
    "One representation choice removes an entire class of bugs: give every CELL a 4-bit WALL MASK (N, E, S, W) and make carving a two-sided operation — knock the wall bit out of BOTH the cell and its neighbour, always. Walls can then never disagree with themselves. On top of that, generation and solving are both STRATEGIES over the same grid: a perfect maze is a spanning tree of the grid graph, so recursive backtracker (a randomised DFS) and randomised Prim or Kruskal (Kruskal resting on union-find) are interchangeable spanning-tree algorithms with visibly different BIAS. Solving is a graph search, and the choice is not stylistic — BFS and A* guarantee the shortest path, DFS finds only *a* path.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: generate a rectangular maze of given width and height as a PERFECT maze — fully connected (every cell reachable from every other) and acyclic (exactly one simple path between any pair of cells), which is precisely the definition of a spanning tree over the grid graph. Generation must be seedable so a given seed always reproduces the same maze, which matters for testing, for shareable puzzles, and for reproducing a reported bug. The system must support MULTIPLE generation algorithms selectable at runtime, because different algorithms produce mazes that FEEL different — long winding corridors versus many short dead ends — and a game needs that control. It must render the maze as text or an image, and it must SOLVE from a start cell to a goal cell, returning the actual path, not just a boolean. The shortest path must be available, and the solver should also report cells explored so different search strategies can be compared and visualised. Extensions that interviewers commonly layer on: braided mazes (deliberately remove some walls to introduce loops, creating multiple routes and making the maze feel less mechanical), weighted terrain, multiple entrances/exits, and non-rectangular grids (hexagonal, or arbitrary masked shapes). Non-functional requirements: generation and solving must be O(cells) or O(cells log cells), because a 500×500 maze is 250,000 cells and an accidental O(n²) — for example, rescanning the whole grid for the next frontier cell — is instantly visible; memory must be O(cells), which rules out storing an explicit adjacency list per cell when a 4-bit mask suffices; and recursion depth must be considered, since a recursive backtracker on 250,000 cells can produce a call chain that overflows the stack, so the iterative explicit-stack form is the correct default. Explicitly in scope as a design goal: adding a new generation or solving algorithm must not require touching the Maze class."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "MAZE owns the grid: width, height, and a flat array of CELLs, plus the neighbour arithmetic (index = row * width + col) and the carve(a, b) operation that is the ONLY way walls are ever removed. Making carving a Maze method rather than something callers do to cells is the invariant guard: no caller can knock down one side of a wall. CELL is deliberately tiny — a row, a column, and a 4-bit WALL MASK where bit 0 is a wall to the north, 1 east, 2 south, 3 west (a byte per cell, so a 500×500 maze is 250KB, not megabytes of objects). Generation algorithms also need a visited marker, which can be a separate boolean array so Cell stays a pure geometry/wall record. DIRECTION is an enum carrying its delta (dRow, dCol), its bit, and its OPPOSITE — the opposite is what makes two-sided carving a one-liner and is the single most useful field in the whole design. MAZEGENERATOR is the Strategy interface: void generate(Maze maze, Random rng). Implementations — RecursiveBacktracker, RandomizedPrim, RandomizedKruskal, Wilson — share nothing but that signature, and each takes the RNG as a parameter so seeding is external and reproducible. MAZESOLVER is the parallel Strategy: Optional<Path> solve(Maze maze, Cell start, Cell goal), returning both the path and the explored set for visualisation. Concrete solvers: BfsSolver, AStarSolver, DfsSolver (kept precisely so the shortest-path difference can be demonstrated). PATH is a value object — the ordered list of cells plus its length. DISJOINTSET is a collaborator used only by the Kruskal generator, which is worth calling out because it shows the maze problem is a thin disguise over classic spanning-tree algorithms. Finally, the maze exposes neighbours(cell) (grid adjacency, ignoring walls — used by generators) and reachableNeighbours(cell) (adjacency filtered by the wall mask — used by solvers): the same grid seen as two different graphs, which is exactly the conceptual pivot the design turns on."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The design is a two-Strategy system over one shared representation, and the reason it is not over-engineering — the usual objection to a Strategy interface — is that BOTH axes have several genuinely different implementations with different observable properties, which is the exact condition Strategy exists for. Generators differ in maze CHARACTER (bias), solvers differ in OPTIMALITY and exploration shape; a switch on an enum inside Maze would couple every algorithm to the data structure and make adding Wilson's algorithm a modification of a class that has nothing to do with it. STRATEGY here also buys testability: any generator can be asserted to produce a perfect maze with the same test — count carved edges (must equal cells − 1, the spanning-tree edge count) and verify full connectivity with one flood fill — which is a beautifully cheap invariant that catches almost every generation bug. The second design decision is the CELL-WITH-WALL-MASK representation over the two obvious alternatives. A separate grid-of-walls (arrays of horizontal and vertical wall booleans, sized (h+1)×w and h×(w+1)) is memory-efficient and is what a renderer naturally wants, but every neighbour query becomes index gymnastics with different formulas per direction, and off-by-one errors at the borders are endemic. A graph of explicit adjacency lists is conceptually clean — the maze IS a graph — but costs far more memory and makes rendering awkward. The bitmask keeps the grid indexable, makes 'can I move north from here?' a single bit test, makes carving symmetric by construction, and serialises to a byte array trivially. The third decision is where randomness lives: the RNG is injected, never created inside an algorithm, so seeding is the caller's concern and every generator is deterministically testable — a small thing that interviewers notice because it is the difference between 'I have written this' and 'I have maintained this'. Patterns beyond Strategy: a light FACTORY mapping an algorithm name to its Strategy for CLI/UI selection, and OBSERVER (a step callback) if the maze is animated — carve-by-carve for generation, visit-by-visit for solving — which costs nothing to design in and is the feature every maze demo eventually wants."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "enum Direction {\n  N(-1, 0, 0b0001), E(0, 1, 0b0010), S(1, 0, 0b0100), W(0, -1, 0b1000);\n  final int dr, dc, bit;\n  Direction(int dr, int dc, int bit) { this.dr = dr; this.dc = dc; this.bit = bit; }\n  Direction opposite() { return values()[(ordinal() + 2) % 4]; }   // N<->S, E<->W\n}\n\nclass Maze {\n  final int width, height;\n  private final byte[] walls;                 // 4 bits per cell: all walls up initially\n  Maze(int w, int h) {\n    width = w; height = h;\n    walls = new byte[w * h];\n    Arrays.fill(walls, (byte) 0b1111);\n  }\n  private int idx(int r, int c) { return r * width + c; }\n  boolean inBounds(int r, int c) { return r >= 0 && r < height && c >= 0 && c < width; }\n  boolean hasWall(int r, int c, Direction d) { return (walls[idx(r, c)] & d.bit) != 0; }\n\n  /** The ONLY mutator: knocking a wall clears the bit on BOTH sides — symmetry by construction. */\n  void carve(int r, int c, Direction d) {\n    int nr = r + d.dr, nc = c + d.dc;\n    if (!inBounds(nr, nc)) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"carve out of bounds\");\n    walls[idx(r, c)]   &= ~d.bit;\n    walls[idx(nr, nc)] &= ~d.opposite().bit;\n  }\n  /** Grid adjacency (generators): neighbours regardless of walls. */\n  List<Direction> openDirections(int r, int c) {\n    List<Direction> out = new ArrayList<>(4);\n    for (Direction d : Direction.values()) if (inBounds(r + d.dr, c + d.dc)) out.add(d);\n    return out;\n  }\n  /** Passage adjacency (solvers): neighbours reachable through a carved wall. */\n  List<int[]> reachable(int r, int c) {\n    List<int[]> out = new ArrayList<>(4);\n    for (Direction d : Direction.values())\n      if (inBounds(r + d.dr, c + d.dc) && !hasWall(r, c, d)) out.add(new int[]{ r + d.dr, c + d.dc });\n    return out;\n  }\n}\n\ninterface MazeGenerator { void generate(Maze m, Random rng); }\n\nclass RecursiveBacktracker implements MazeGenerator {   // randomised DFS, ITERATIVE\n  public void generate(Maze m, Random rng) {\n    boolean[] visited = new boolean[m.width * m.height];\n    Deque<int[]> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    stack.push(new int[]{ 0, 0 });\n    visited[0] = true;\n    while (!stack.isEmpty()) {\n      int[] cur = stack.peek();\n      List<Direction> candidates = new ArrayList<>();\n      for (Direction d : m.openDirections(cur[0], cur[1]))\n        if (!visited[(cur[0] + d.dr) * m.width + (cur[1] + d.dc)]) candidates.add(d);\n      if (candidates.isEmpty()) { stack.pop(); continue; }        // backtrack\n      Direction d = candidates.get(rng.nextInt(candidates.size()));\n      m.carve(cur[0], cur[1], d);\n      int nr = cur[0] + d.dr, nc = cur[1] + d.dc;\n      visited[nr * m.width + nc] = true;\n      stack.push(new int[]{ nr, nc });\n    }\n  }\n}\n\ninterface MazeSolver { Optional<List<int[]>> solve(Maze m, int[] start, int[] goal); }\n\nclass BfsSolver implements MazeSolver {                 // shortest path, unweighted\n  public Optional<List<int[]>> solve(Maze m, int[] start, int[] goal) {\n    int n = m.width * m.height;\n    int[] parent = new int[n];\n    Arrays.fill(parent, -1);\n    boolean[] seen = new boolean[n];\n    Deque<int[]> q = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    q.add(start); seen[start[0] * m.width + start[1]] = true;\n    while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n      int[] cur = q.poll();\n      if (cur[0] == goal[0] && cur[1] == goal[1]) return Optional.of(rebuild(parent, m, start, goal));\n      for (int[] nb : m.reachable(cur[0], cur[1])) {\n        int ni = nb[0] * m.width + nb[1];\n        if (seen[ni]) continue;\n        seen[ni] = true;\n        parent[ni] = cur[0] * m.width + cur[1];       // first time seen == shortest\n        q.add(nb);\n      }\n    }\n    return Optional.empty();\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: generation algorithms as Strategies — spanning trees and their bias",
        "The unifying insight, and the one that turns this from a puzzle into a design question, is that a perfect maze IS A SPANNING TREE of the grid graph: cells are vertices, potential passages are edges, and a maze that is connected with exactly one path between any two cells is by definition a tree covering all vertices — hence exactly cells − 1 carved passages. Every generation algorithm is therefore a randomised spanning-tree algorithm, and the interesting differences are in the DISTRIBUTION of trees each one produces, which is visible to a player as 'bias'. RECURSIVE BACKTRACKER (randomised DFS): walk to a random unvisited neighbour, carving as you go; when stuck, backtrack along the stack to the most recent cell that still has an unvisited neighbour. Because it always extends the CURRENT path as far as it can, it produces long, snaking corridors, few but long dead ends, and a high 'river' factor — mazes that feel like a single winding journey. It is O(cells), needs a visited array plus a stack, and the recursive form must be converted to the explicit-stack form shown in the code, because the stack depth is the length of the longest corridor and can approach the cell count — a 500×500 maze can blow a default JVM stack, which is a concrete, checkable claim rather than hand-waving. RANDOMIZED PRIM: maintain a FRONTIER of walls adjacent to the visited region, repeatedly pick one at random, and carve it if it leads to an unvisited cell. Because it grows outward from the seed in all directions rather than following a single path, it produces many SHORT dead ends and a bushy, uniformly textured maze — the opposite bias. Naive implementations rescan the whole frontier list to remove entries, making it O(cells²); with a proper frontier collection and lazy deletion of stale entries it is O(cells log cells) or effectively linear. RANDOMIZED KRUSKAL: take ALL potential walls, shuffle them, and carve each one whose two cells are in DIFFERENT components — which is exactly Kruskal's minimum spanning tree with random weights, and the component test is a DISJOINT SET (union-find) with union by rank and path compression, giving near-linear total time. It produces a texture similar to Prim's, and it is the algorithm that makes the connection to classic graph theory undeniable — worth naming in an interview because it shows you see the maze as a graph problem, not a grid puzzle. WILSON'S ALGORITHM (loop-erased random walks) is the one that produces a UNIFORM random spanning tree — every possible maze equally likely, no bias at all — at the cost of slow early progress; mention it as the answer to 'can you generate an unbiased maze?', which is a real follow-up. The design payoff of Strategy is that all four are drop-in: the Maze class, the renderer, and every solver are untouched, and the same perfect-maze invariant test validates all of them."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: representation — the wall bitmask and why carving must be two-sided",
        "The representation decision is where implementations quietly go wrong, and it is worth dissecting because the bug it prevents is one every first-time implementer hits. Consider storing walls per cell WITHOUT enforcing symmetry: cell (3,4) records 'no wall to the east' while cell (3,5) still records 'wall to the west'. Now the maze is a directed graph by accident: a solver walking east from (3,4) passes through, and the same solver walking west from (3,5) is blocked. The path found depends on the direction of travel, the renderer draws a wall the solver walks through, and the bug is invisible in small hand-tested cases. The fix is structural, not disciplinary: make carve(cell, direction) the ONLY mutator, and have it clear the bit in the cell AND the opposite bit in the neighbour, which is what Direction.opposite() exists for. No caller can then create an asymmetric wall, because no caller has access to the raw bytes. This is the general principle worth articulating — when an invariant spans two objects, put the mutation on the owner that can see both, rather than trusting every caller to remember. Now the alternatives, because the interviewer will ask. GRID-OF-WALLS: two separate boolean arrays, horizontal walls of size (height+1)×width and vertical walls of size height×(width+1). Each wall is stored exactly ONCE, so asymmetry is impossible by construction too, and it maps directly onto how a renderer draws lines — genuinely a good choice for rendering-heavy code. Its cost is that neighbour logic branches per direction (moving north tests horizontal[r][c], moving south tests horizontal[r+1][c]) and the boundary indices are a fertile source of off-by-one errors; the bitmask keeps one uniform formula for all four directions. EXPLICIT GRAPH: a Map<Cell, List<Cell>> of passages. Conceptually the truest model and it generalises immediately to hexagonal or irregular mazes, but for a rectangular grid it burns an object and a list per cell for information a nibble encodes, and loses the O(1) coordinate indexing that makes rendering and A* heuristics simple. The bitmask's other virtues are worth stating: a whole maze is a byte[] that serialises, hashes, and diffs trivially (so a maze can be shared as a short string); the walls of a cell can be tested with a single AND; and 'is this cell a dead end?' is a POPCOUNT of the mask equal to three, which makes braiding — removing walls from dead ends to create loops — a two-line post-process. That last point matters because braiding is the standard follow-up: a perfect maze has exactly one solution and can feel mechanical, so games deliberately carve a percentage of dead-end walls to create loops, converting the spanning tree into a general graph and making the solver's optimality question suddenly meaningful."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: solving — BFS vs A*, and why DFS finds a path but not the shortest",
        "Solving is graph search on the passage graph, and the choice of search is a correctness decision, not a taste one. DFS finds A path and finds it quickly in terms of code, but the path it returns is whatever the first successful descent happened to be: in a perfect maze there is exactly one simple path between two cells, so DFS-with-backtracking will eventually return it — but only if it returns the path along the current stack at the moment the goal is found, and in a BRAIDED maze (loops present) or a maze with multiple exits it returns an arbitrary route that can be many times longer than optimal, which is exactly the 900-versus-140 symptom in the prompt. State this crisply: DFS explores deep before wide, and the first goal it reaches is the first one its arbitrary ordering led to, with no relationship to distance. BFS explores in order of increasing distance from the start, so the FIRST time it reaches any cell is via a shortest path — that is the loop invariant, and the parent pointer recorded at first discovery is what the path reconstruction walks backwards. In an unweighted grid maze, BFS is optimal, O(cells) in time, and O(cells) in memory for the frontier and the parent array; it explores in a growing diamond around the start, which is visually obvious and, in a large maze where the goal is far away, wasteful — it expands cells in every direction, including directly away from the goal. A* fixes exactly that waste by ordering the frontier by f = g + h, where g is the cost so far and h is a heuristic estimate of the remaining distance. On a 4-connected grid the natural heuristic is MANHATTAN distance, |dr| + |dc|, and the property that makes it safe is ADMISSIBILITY: it never overestimates the true remaining cost, because any real route must cover at least that many row and column steps and walls can only make it longer. An admissible (and here also consistent) heuristic guarantees A* returns an optimal path while typically expanding far fewer cells than BFS, since the priority queue pulls cells that head toward the goal first. Two precise cautions: Euclidean distance is admissible on a 4-connected grid but weaker (it underestimates more, so it prunes less), while diagonal or scaled heuristics can OVERESTIMATE and silently break optimality — an inadmissible heuristic turns A* into a fast, wrong search, and knowing that distinction is the point of the question. Also note that with h ≡ 0, A* degenerates exactly into Dijkstra, which on a uniform-cost graph behaves like BFS — a nice way to show the three algorithms are one family. Implementation details that earn credit: reconstruct the path from parent pointers rather than storing a path per frontier entry (which multiplies memory by path length); use a visited set to avoid re-expansion; and for the priority queue, either keep a position map for decrease-key or use the lazy approach of pushing improved entries and skipping stale pops — the same trade that appears in every Dijkstra implementation. Finally, bidirectional BFS is the cheap win for large mazes with distant endpoints: search from both ends and stop when the frontiers meet, cutting explored area roughly from O(b^d) to O(2·b^(d/2))."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "GENERATOR CHOICE is a product decision expressed as bias: recursive backtracker for long, dramatic corridors and simple O(cells) code (iterative form mandatory at scale); randomised Prim or Kruskal for a bushier maze with many short dead ends — Kruskal when you already have a union-find and want the MST framing, Prim when you want to grow from a seed; Wilson when unbiased uniformity actually matters (research, or a puzzle game that must not have a recognisable signature), accepting slower generation. Eller's algorithm deserves a mention as the one that generates row by row in O(1) memory per row, which is how you make an infinite scrolling maze. SOLVER CHOICE: BFS when the maze is unweighted and you want the simplest correct shortest path; A* with Manhattan when the maze is large and the endpoints are far apart, remembering that the heuristic must stay admissible; Dijkstra the moment cells acquire weights (mud, doors, teleports); DFS only when any path will do and memory is tight, since its frontier is O(depth) rather than O(width of the search). BITMASK vs GRID-OF-WALLS vs GRAPH: bitmask for uniform neighbour logic, compact serialisation, and symmetric carving; grid-of-walls when rendering and wall-level editing dominate; explicit graph when the topology stops being a rectangular grid — hexagonal mazes, masked shapes, or 3D/multi-level mazes with stairs, where the bitmask's fixed four directions stop fitting and the Strategy interfaces survive unchanged only if generators are written against a neighbours() abstraction rather than against Direction.values(). That last point is the real extension-point lesson: if you expect non-rectangular mazes, the generators should depend on the Maze's adjacency methods, not on the enum. Other extensions: BRAIDING (remove a fraction of dead-end walls) turns the tree into a graph with loops, making multiple solutions exist and giving the BFS-versus-DFS optimality difference real consequences; WEIGHTED terrain moves solving to Dijkstra/A* with real costs; MULTIPLE EXITS turn the solve into a multi-target BFS (seed the queue with all goals and search backwards); a difficulty metric can be derived cheaply from the solution length and the number of decision points along it, which is more meaningful than maze size. WHAT BREAKS AT SCALE: nothing algorithmically — everything is near-linear — but memory becomes the constraint at millions of cells, which pushes toward chunked generation with a deterministic per-chunk seed so an infinite maze is reproducible without being stored, and toward streaming solvers that never materialise the whole grid."),
    ],
    "Maze generation and solving is a favourite because it looks like a toy and grades real fundamentals: whether you recognise a perfect maze as a spanning tree (and can therefore name recursive backtracker, Prim, and Kruskal as randomised spanning-tree algorithms with different bias, with Kruskal resting on union-find), whether you pick a representation that makes the two-sided wall invariant structural rather than a thing callers must remember, and whether you know that DFS returns a path while BFS and admissible-heuristic A* return the shortest one. Interviewers probe with 'why is my maze passable one way only?', 'why does every corridor snake?', 'my path is six times too long', and 'make it faster on a 500×500 grid' — questions that map exactly onto the wall mask, generator bias, search optimality, and the iterative-versus-recursive stack depth. The Strategy structure is what makes all four answers coexist in one design instead of one switch.",
    [
      G("one_sided_wall", "Storing walls per cell but clearing only the current cell's bit when carving, so neighbouring cells disagree about the wall between them.", "Make carve(cell, direction) the only mutator and have it clear the bit in the cell AND the opposite bit in the neighbour via Direction.opposite(); alternatively store each wall once in a grid-of-walls so asymmetry is impossible.", "A passage traversable in one direction only; a renderer drawing a wall the solver walks through; wall state mutated directly from generator code rather than through a maze method."),
      G("dfs_shortest_path", "Using DFS (or 'any search') to solve and reporting the result as the shortest path.", "Use BFS for unweighted mazes — first discovery is via a shortest path — or A* with an admissible heuristic such as Manhattan distance; reconstruct from parent pointers, and use Dijkstra once cells are weighted.", "A returned path many times longer than optimal in a braided maze; a solver with a stack instead of a queue described as 'shortest'; a heuristic that overestimates (scaled or diagonal on a 4-connected grid) breaking A* optimality silently."),
      G("recursive_generator_depth", "Writing the recursive backtracker as actual recursion and treating all generators as equivalent, with no view on bias or complexity.", "Use an explicit stack — depth approaches the cell count on large mazes — and choose the generator by the bias you want: backtracker for long corridors, Prim/Kruskal for many short dead ends (Kruskal via union-find), Wilson for an unbiased uniform spanning tree.", "StackOverflowError on a large grid; a randomised Prim implementation rescanning the whole frontier each step (O(cells²)); no test asserting cells − 1 carved edges and full connectivity."),
    ],
    0.4, DIAG.maze, "Maze generator and solver class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── SCRABBLE ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m16", "lld_m16_t8", 8, "Design Scrabble", "design-scrabble",
    ["case-study", "board-game", "validation", "trie"],
    "A player lays QUIZ down column 8, crossing three existing words. Your engine scores 66 and accepts it — but one of the perpendicular words it created is QI…ZO, which is not a word, and the triple-word square under the Q was already used two turns ago. Design Scrabble so that both bugs are structurally impossible.",
    "Everyone reaches for the board and the tile bag first; the difficulty is somewhere else entirely. It is MOVE VALIDATION — and specifically the CROSS-WORDS. A placement forms one main word along the direction of play and, potentially, one perpendicular word at EVERY tile that has a neighbour on the other axis. All of them must be legal, and forgetting them is the single most common failure in a candidate's design. Validation runs before scoring, scoring walks every formed word applying letter multipliers first and word multipliers after, premium squares are consumed once and never again, and the dictionary is a trie so both lookup and AI move generation share one structure.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: two to four players share a 15×15 BOARD with a fixed layout of PREMIUM SQUARES — double and triple letter, double and triple word — and a centre star. A TILE BAG holds 100 tiles with the standard letter distribution and point values, plus two blanks worth zero that may stand for any letter. Each player holds a RACK of up to seven tiles, refilled from the bag after every turn. On a turn a player may PLACE tiles to form a word, EXCHANGE tiles (if the bag holds at least seven), or PASS. A placement must satisfy every rule: all placed tiles lie in a single row or column, they must be contiguous with existing tiles in that line (gaps may be filled by tiles already on the board but not left empty), the first move of the game must cover the centre square, every subsequent move must touch at least one existing tile, and EVERY word formed — the main word plus each perpendicular cross-word — must be in the dictionary. Scoring: each formed word's value is the sum of its letters' values with letter multipliers applied to newly placed tiles, then multiplied by any word multipliers from newly covered squares; every formed word is scored, premium squares count only on the turn they are covered, and using all seven rack tiles adds a fifty-point BINGO. The game ends when the bag is empty and a player uses their last tile (that player gains the sum of opponents' remaining racks, each opponent loses theirs) or after six consecutive scoreless turns. Challenges may invalidate a play. Non-functional: dictionary lookup happens many times per turn and must be O(word length), not a linear scan of 180,000 entries; the whole word list must fit comfortably in memory; validation must be a PURE function of (board, placement) so a move can be checked, scored, and previewed without mutating anything — which also makes undo and challenge trivial; and if an AI opponent is in scope, move generation must produce a strong move in well under a second, which rules out enumerating rack permutations against every square."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "TILE is an immutable value: a letter, a point value, and an isBlank flag; a placed blank carries the letter it was designated as but keeps a value of zero, a distinction that must live in the model or scoring will quietly overpay. SQUARE holds a position, a premium type (NONE, DL, TL, DW, TW), the tile currently on it, and — critically — a boolean recording whether the premium has been CONSUMED; making consumption a field on the square rather than something the scorer remembers is what makes the once-only rule structural. BOARD owns the 15×15 grid of Squares, exposes tileAt(row, col), isEmpty(), and the traversal helpers that walk in a direction until an empty square is reached — those walkers are what both validation and scoring are built from, so they belong on the Board. RACK holds up to seven tiles with the ability to check that a placement is actually supported by tiles the player holds. TILEBAG owns the remaining tiles and the shuffle/draw/exchange operations, seeded for reproducibility. MOVE is the request, deliberately expressed as a set of (row, col, tile) placements plus a derived direction — not as a word string, because the same word can be formed in ways that differ in which tiles are new, and everything (scoring, multipliers, cross-words) depends on WHICH tiles are new. WORD is a derived value: the letters, their squares, and which of them were newly placed this turn. MOVEVALIDATOR is the heart: it takes a board and a move and returns either the list of formed Words or a specific rejection reason; it mutates nothing. DICTIONARY is a trie (or DAWG) exposing contains(word) and — the part that matters for AI — prefix navigation. SCORER consumes the validated list of Words and the set of newly covered squares and returns points. MOVEGENERATOR (the AI) uses anchors and cross-checks over the same board and dictionary. GAME is the turn loop and state machine: turn order, scoreless-turn counter, end conditions, and final adjustments."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The design's spine is a strict pipeline: PARSE the move into placements, VALIDATE geometry, EXTRACT all formed words, VALIDATE every word against the dictionary, SCORE, then COMMIT. Each stage is pure except the last, and that purity is the design's most valuable property — a move can be previewed for a hint system, evaluated by an AI thousands of times per turn, undone after a successful challenge, or replayed from a game log, all with the same code path, because nothing before commit touches the board. Candidates who interleave scoring with placement (adding points as they set tiles down) lose all of that and get the premium-square bugs for free. The second spine decision is that WORD EXTRACTION is a distinct step from word VALIDATION. Extraction is pure geometry: walk from the placement in the play direction to both ends to build the main word, then for each placed tile walk perpendicular to both ends and, if the resulting run has length ≥ 2, that is a cross-word. Validation then simply asks the dictionary about each extracted word. Keeping them separate means the cross-word rule cannot be forgotten in one place and remembered in another — there is exactly one function that says what words a move forms, and scoring consumes the same list, so scoring and validation can never disagree about which words exist. Patterns, each earning its place: STRATEGY for the dictionary (trie versus DAWG versus a hash set) and for the AI's move-choice policy (highest score, or score minus a rack-leave penalty); COMMAND for a Move so it can be applied and undone, which is exactly what challenges and undo need; a light STATE machine for the turn/game lifecycle; and a FACTORY or config file for the board layout and tile distribution so a house-rules variant or a different language edition is data, not code — a genuinely realistic requirement, since Scrabble has different tile distributions per language. What to avoid: putting scoring logic on Square subclasses (a DoubleWordSquare class with a score method) — multipliers apply to whole words, not to squares in isolation, so square-local scoring cannot express the rule correctly; the premium type is data the Scorer reads, not behaviour the square owns. Say that; it is a case where the polymorphic instinct produces a wrong model."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "record Placement(int row, int col, Tile tile) {}\n\nclass MoveValidator {\n  private final Board board; private final Dictionary dict;\n  MoveValidator(Board b, Dictionary d) { board = b; dict = d; }\n\n  /** Pure: returns every word the move forms, or throws with a precise reason. */\n  List<Word> validate(List<Placement> ps, boolean firstMove) {\n    if (ps.isEmpty()) throw new InvalidMove(\"no tiles placed\");\n    boolean sameRow = ps.stream().map(Placement::row).distinct().count() == 1;\n    boolean sameCol = ps.stream().map(Placement::col).distinct().count() == 1;\n    if (!sameRow && !sameCol) throw new InvalidMove(\"tiles must share a row or column\");\n    Dir dir = sameRow ? Dir.ACROSS : Dir.DOWN;\n    for (Placement p : ps)\n      if (board.tileAt(p.row(), p.col()) != null) throw new InvalidMove(\"square occupied\");\n\n    // Contiguity: every square between the first and last placement must be filled\n    // either by this move or by a tile already on the board (no holes).\n    int lo = ps.stream().mapToInt(p -> dir == Dir.ACROSS ? p.col() : p.row()).min().getAsInt();\n    int hi = ps.stream().mapToInt(p -> dir == Dir.ACROSS ? p.col() : p.row()).max().getAsInt();\n    int fixed = dir == Dir.ACROSS ? ps.get(0).row() : ps.get(0).col();\n    for (int i = lo; i <= hi; i++)\n      if (!occupiedAfter(ps, dir, fixed, i)) throw new InvalidMove(\"gap in placement\");\n\n    if (firstMove) {\n      if (ps.stream().noneMatch(p -> p.row() == 7 && p.col() == 7))\n        throw new InvalidMove(\"first move must cover the centre\");\n    } else if (ps.stream().noneMatch(p -> board.hasAdjacentTile(p.row(), p.col()))) {\n      throw new InvalidMove(\"move must connect to an existing tile\");\n    }\n\n    List<Word> words = new ArrayList<>();\n    Word main = board.readWord(ps, dir, fixed, lo, hi);        // main word, incl. existing tiles\n    if (main.length() >= 2) words.add(main);\n    for (Placement p : ps) {                                    // THE STEP CANDIDATES FORGET\n      Word cross = board.readCross(p, dir.perpendicular());\n      if (cross.length() >= 2) words.add(cross);\n    }\n    if (words.isEmpty()) throw new InvalidMove(\"no word formed\");\n    for (Word w : words)\n      if (!dict.contains(w.letters())) throw new InvalidMove(\"not a word: \" + w.letters());\n    return words;\n  }\n}\n\nclass Scorer {\n  int score(List<Word> words, Set<Position> newlyPlaced) {\n    int total = 0;\n    for (Word w : words) {\n      int wordScore = 0, wordMultiplier = 1;\n      for (SquareLetter sl : w.letters2()) {\n        int v = sl.tile().value();                        // blanks are worth 0\n        if (newlyPlaced.contains(sl.position()) && !sl.square().premiumUsed()) {\n          switch (sl.square().premium()) {                // LETTER multipliers first...\n            case DL -> v *= 2;\n            case TL -> v *= 3;\n            case DW -> wordMultiplier *= 2;               // ...WORD multipliers accumulate\n            case TW -> wordMultiplier *= 3;\n            default -> { }\n          }\n        }\n        wordScore += v;\n      }\n      total += wordScore * wordMultiplier;                // applied AFTER the letter sum\n    }\n    if (newlyPlaced.size() == 7) total += 50;             // bingo\n    return total;\n  }\n}\n\nclass TrieDictionary implements Dictionary {\n  private final Node root = new Node();\n  private static final class Node { final Node[] next = new Node[26]; boolean word; }\n  public void add(String w) {\n    Node n = root;\n    for (char c : w.toCharArray()) {\n      int i = c - 'A';\n      if (n.next[i] == null) n.next[i] = new Node();\n      n = n.next[i];\n    }\n    n.word = true;\n  }\n  public boolean contains(String w) { Node n = walk(w); return n != null && n.word; }\n  public boolean hasPrefix(String p) { return walk(p) != null; }   // prunes AI generation\n  private Node walk(String s) {\n    Node n = root;\n    for (char c : s.toCharArray()) { n = n.next[c - 'A']; if (n == null) return null; }\n    return n;\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: move validation — contiguity, connection, and the cross-words everyone forgets",
        "Validation is where this design is won or lost, and it decomposes into four checks that must all pass, in an order that produces useful error messages. ALIGNMENT: every newly placed tile must share a row or share a column; a single tile is ambiguous and is treated as both, which matters because a lone tile can extend an across word, a down word, or both at once. CONTIGUITY: between the lowest and highest coordinate of the placement along the play axis, every intermediate square must be occupied — by a new tile or by one already on the board. This is the check that permits the common and legal move of playing C and T around an existing A to form CAT, while rejecting a placement with a genuine hole. Implement it as a scan over the closed interval rather than by sorting placements and comparing adjacent pairs, because the latter cannot see existing tiles filling the gaps. CONNECTION: after the first move, at least one newly placed tile must be orthogonally adjacent to an existing tile — otherwise the player has started a second, floating board. The first move is the special case: it must cover the centre square, and note that this is not merely a rule but the base case that makes the connection rule well-founded (with an empty board there is nothing to connect to). Now the check that actually separates candidates: CROSS-WORDS. When tiles are laid down the across direction, each of them may sit adjacent — above or below — to existing tiles, and if it does, a perpendicular word is formed at that column. A single move can form one main word and up to seven cross-words, and EVERY ONE of them must be a legal dictionary word. This is exactly the bug in the prompt: QUIZ itself is fine, and the engine that validates only the main word accepts a move that created gibberish perpendicular to it. The correct extraction is mechanical: for each placed tile, walk perpendicular from that square in both directions until an empty square, and if the resulting run has length at least two, it is a formed word. Doing this for the main word too (walking along the play axis past the placement to include existing tiles at both ends) means the main word is often longer than what the player typed — another routine source of bugs, since players extend existing words. Two design consequences follow. First, extraction must return the words WITH their squares and a marker for which letters are new, because scoring needs exactly that and re-deriving it separately is how scoring and validation drift apart. Second, validation must be pure and total: it either returns the full word list or a specific reason, and it must not partially mutate the board on the way, because a rejected move has to leave the game untouched and a challenge has to be able to re-run the same validation after the fact. A final subtlety worth volunteering: BLANKS must be designated at placement time (the player declares what letter the blank represents), because the dictionary check needs a concrete letter — but the tile's value stays zero forever, including on a double-letter square, where doubling zero is still zero."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: scoring — multiplier order, once-only premiums, and every formed word",
        "Scoring has three rules that interact, and getting the interaction right is the difference between a plausible implementation and a correct one. RULE ONE — EVERY FORMED WORD SCORES. The main word and each cross-word are scored independently and summed; a tile that participates in two words contributes to both. This is why scoring consumes the validator's word list rather than recomputing anything: the set of words is defined once. A move that adds one tile to make a five-letter main word and a four-letter cross-word scores both in full, which is how large scores are actually built in real play. RULE TWO — ORDER OF MULTIPLIERS. Within a word, letter multipliers apply to individual tile values FIRST, and the word multipliers apply to the resulting SUM afterwards. Applying them in the wrong order — or, worse, applying a word multiplier to a single tile — produces silently wrong scores that are hard to spot because they are in the right ballpark. If a word covers two word-multiplier squares, the multipliers COMPOUND (a double-word and a triple-word on the same word give ×6), which is a rule people frequently implement as an addition instead of a product. And when one move forms several words that each cover a word-multiplier square, that multiplier applies to each word it participates in — a tile on a triple-word square triples both the main word and its cross-word, which is where the famous huge scores come from. RULE THREE — PREMIUMS ARE CONSUMED. A premium square counts only on the turn the tile covering it is placed. On every later turn, a word running through that square uses the tile's face value only. This is the second bug in the prompt, and the structural fix is to store the consumed flag on the SQUARE and to have the scorer's multiplier logic gated on both 'this position is newly placed this turn' and 'this square's premium is unused', with the commit step marking covered premiums used. Notice how the design makes this safe: because scoring receives the set of newly placed positions from the validated move, it cannot accidentally apply a multiplier to an existing tile — a scorer that walks a word and applies whatever premium it finds under each letter is the classic wrong implementation, and it double-counts every time someone extends an old word. THE BINGO: using all seven rack tiles in one move adds fifty points, added AFTER all word scores and never multiplied. Finally, END-GAME ADJUSTMENTS are part of scoring and easy to omit: when a player uses their last tile with an empty bag, they add the sum of every opponent's remaining rack values and each opponent subtracts their own; if play ends by six consecutive scoreless turns instead, everyone simply subtracts their rack. Implementing scoring as a pure function of (words, newlyPlaced) is what makes all of this testable with hand-verified fixtures — and hand-verified fixtures from real games are the only way this code is ever proven right."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: dictionary structure and AI move generation via anchors and cross-checks",
        "THE DICTIONARY. A hash set of 180,000 words answers contains() in O(1) and is perfectly adequate for validating human moves — say that, because reaching for a trie purely for lookup is over-engineering. The reason to build a TRIE is PREFIX NAVIGATION: an AI extending a partial word needs to know 'is ABQ the start of any word?' so it can abandon that branch immediately, and only a trie answers that. Memory-wise, the trie shares prefixes; a DAWG (directed acyclic word graph, a minimised trie that also shares SUFFIXES) compresses the same word list from tens of megabytes to a few, which is why every serious Scrabble engine uses one — the classic GADDAG variant additionally stores each word with reversed prefixes so a generator can start from any letter and expand in both directions, which is what makes generation from anchor squares fast. Naming the trie → DAWG → GADDAG progression, and the reason for each step, is exactly the kind of depth this topic exists to test. MOVE GENERATION. The naive AI is unusable and it is worth saying why with numbers: seven rack tiles give 7! = 5,040 orderings, times subsets, times 15×15 squares, times two directions, and each candidate still needs full validation — millions of dictionary calls per turn. The standard algorithm (Appel and Jacobson) reduces this enormously with two ideas. ANCHOR SQUARES: any legal non-first move must touch an existing tile, so only empty squares orthogonally adjacent to an occupied square can be the connection point of a new word. On a typical mid-game board that is a few dozen squares instead of 225, and each one bounds how far a word may extend left before running into another tile. CROSS-CHECKS: for each empty square, precompute the SET OF LETTERS that would form a legal perpendicular word there, given the tiles already above and below — a 26-bit mask per square, computed once per turn in one pass over the board. During generation, placing a letter is then a single bit test rather than a dictionary call, and any branch that would create an illegal cross-word dies immediately instead of being discovered at validation time. Combine the two with a trie/DAWG walk — extend left within the allowed span, then extend right through the anchor while both the dictionary node and the cross-check mask permit the letter — and a full move enumeration takes milliseconds. Blanks are handled by trying all 26 substitutions on that branch (expensive but bounded, and the reason engines often generate blank-free moves first). The final piece is MOVE CHOICE, which is not the same as generation: the highest-scoring move is a weak strategy because it ignores what it leaves on the rack and what it opens for the opponent. Real engines score each candidate as points plus a RACK LEAVE evaluation (keeping a balanced vowel/consonant mix, avoiding a Q with no U, valuing the S and blank tiles highly) minus a board-openness penalty for handing the opponent a triple-word lane. That separation — generate all legal moves, then rank them with a pluggable evaluation Strategy — is the design point: it keeps the correctness-critical generator independent of the strategy heuristics, which will be tuned forever."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "DICTIONARY: HASH SET vs TRIE vs DAWG. Hash set is simplest and fastest for pure validation and is the right answer when there is no AI; the trie earns its complexity only when prefix queries are needed; the DAWG is the memory-optimal form for a fixed word list (built offline, loaded as a flat array) and the GADDAG is the specialised structure for bidirectional generation — pick by whether you need lookup, generation, or generation-at-speed, and be able to say that upgrading is a change behind the Dictionary interface, not a redesign. Note also that the word list itself is a configuration concern: TWL versus SOWPODS versus non-English lists must be swappable, and challenge rules differ by tournament, so both belong in data. BOARD REPRESENTATION: a 15×15 array of Square objects is clear and cheap at this size; bitboards and packed letter arrays matter only for an engine simulating millions of positions, and saying 'not needed at 225 squares' is better judgement than premature optimisation. VALIDATION PURITY: validating against an unmutated board plus a placement list costs a little extra traversal compared with 'place the tiles, then check, then roll back on failure', but rollback-based designs are how partial mutations leak on the error path — and purity is what enables preview, hints, AI simulation, undo, and challenge resolution to share one implementation. STORING THE MOVE AS PLACEMENTS vs AS A WORD STRING: placements are strictly more informative (which tiles are new determines multipliers and cross-words), and a word string is a lossy encoding that cannot express the common case of building through existing tiles. AI STRENGTH vs TIME: highest-score-only is a few lines on top of generation and plays a recognisable, mediocre game; adding rack-leave evaluation is the single biggest strength gain per line of code; full simulation of opponent replies (Monte Carlo over plausible racks) is what top engines do and costs orders of magnitude more time — the Strategy seam on move choice is what lets all three coexist. EXTENSIONS: challenges become natural because validation is pure and a Move is a Command — re-run the validator, and on success or failure apply the configured penalty; multiplayer over a network needs the server to be the sole validator (never trust a client-computed score) and benefits from the same pure function; variants — different board layouts, different tile distributions, Super Scrabble's 21×21 board — are all data if the layout and distribution are loaded rather than hardcoded, which is the concrete test of whether the design is really extensible."),
    ],
    "Scrabble is a deceptive prompt: candidates spend their time on the tile bag and the board grid, and the interviewer is waiting to ask 'what words did that move form?'. The graded content is move VALIDATION — alignment, contiguity through existing tiles, connection to the board, the first-move-through-centre base case, and above all the perpendicular CROSS-WORDS that a placement creates and that must every one be legal — followed by scoring that applies letter multipliers before word multipliers, compounds word multipliers, scores every formed word, and consumes premium squares exactly once. The dictionary question separates 'hash set is fine for lookup' from 'a trie or DAWG is required for prefix-driven AI generation', and the AI question rewards anchors plus cross-check bitmasks over brute-force permutation. Keeping validation and scoring pure functions over (board, placements) is what makes preview, undo, challenges, and AI simulation one code path instead of four.",
    [
      G("missing_cross_words", "Validating and scoring only the main word, ignoring the perpendicular words a placement creates at every tile with a neighbour on the other axis.", "Extract words in one place: walk the play axis for the main word (including existing tiles at both ends), then walk perpendicular from every placed tile and treat any run of length ≥ 2 as a formed word; validate and score all of them from that single list.", "A move accepted that creates a non-word perpendicular to the main word; scoring that misses the cross-word points; validation and scoring each deriving their own idea of which words exist."),
      G("premium_reuse_and_order", "Applying premium squares whenever a word crosses them, and applying word multipliers to individual letters or summing them instead of compounding.", "Gate multipliers on both 'newly placed this turn' and an unused flag stored on the Square, marked consumed at commit; apply letter multipliers to tile values first, sum the word, then multiply by the compounded word multipliers, adding the 50-point bingo afterwards.", "Extending an old word re-triggers a triple-word square; DW+TW computed as ×5 instead of ×6; a word multiplier applied inside the per-letter loop; blanks scoring points on a double-letter square."),
      G("bruteforce_ai_and_dictionary", "Generating AI moves by permuting the rack against every square, and treating the dictionary as interchangeable with a hash set regardless of use.", "Restrict candidates to anchor squares adjacent to existing tiles, precompute a 26-bit cross-check mask per empty square so illegal cross-words are pruned by a bit test, and walk a trie/DAWG (GADDAG for bidirectional expansion); rank the generated moves with a separate evaluation Strategy including rack leave.", "Turn times measured in seconds; millions of dictionary lookups per turn; no prefix pruning; the AI always playing the highest-scoring move and keeping an unplayable rack."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.scrabble, "Scrabble class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── HEAP / PRIORITY QUEUE ────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m17", "lld_m17_t1", 1, "Design a Heap / Priority Queue", "design-heap-priority-queue",
    ["case-study", "data-structure", "algorithms", "api-design"],
    "Build a priority queue: push, peek, pop, plus build-from-array. Then the interviewer says 'now use it for Dijkstra' — and your API has no way to say 'the distance to node 47 just improved, move it up'. You cannot even FIND node 47 in the heap. What does the API have to look like, and what does it cost?",
    "A binary heap is a COMPLETE binary tree flattened into an array, so parent and child links are arithmetic — parent(i) = (i−1)/2, children 2i+1 and 2i+2 — and there are no node objects at all. Two primitives, SIFT-UP and SIFT-DOWN, are the only real code; push, pop, heapify, and decrease-key are all compositions of them. The design content is the CONTRACT: a heap gives you O(1) access to the minimum and O(log n) insert and extract, but no search, no ordered iteration, and — the one that bites — no way to locate an arbitrary element. Decrease-key therefore forces an explicit choice: carry a position map updated on every swap, or push duplicates and skip stale pops.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: a PriorityQueue<E> that maintains a collection ordered by priority and supports push(e) to insert, peek() to read the highest-priority element without removing it, pop() to remove and return it, size() and isEmpty(). Ordering is supplied by a Comparator (or by E being Comparable), so the same structure serves as a min-heap or a max-heap without duplicating code — reversing a comparator is the whole difference, and a design that hardcodes < is failing a requirement, not an aesthetic. Bulk construction from an existing array or collection must be supported and must be efficient, because the difference between building in O(n) and O(n log n) is a real and frequently examined distinction. The queue must grow beyond any initial capacity, and it must reject or clearly define behaviour for null elements and for peek/pop on an empty queue (returning an Optional, or throwing — pick one and be consistent). ADVANCED OPERATIONS that turn the structure from a toy into a usable component: decreaseKey/increaseKey (an element's priority changes in place, and the structure must restore its invariant), remove(e) for an arbitrary element, and merge of two queues. Each of these is where the naive design fails, and the requirements section is the right place to flag that they need extra machinery. Non-functional: push and pop must be O(log n) worst case, peek O(1), and build O(n); memory overhead must be small — an array-backed heap stores essentially just the elements, unlike a node-based tree with two child pointers and a parent pointer per element; and the structure must be usable as the frontier in Dijkstra and A*, which is the concrete workload that exposes the decrease-key problem. What is explicitly NOT required, and must be stated as such because callers will assume otherwise: ordered iteration (iterating a heap array yields no meaningful order beyond the root), O(1) search, and stability between equal-priority elements."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "The object model is deliberately minimal — this is a data structure, and the design points are the contract and the invariant, not a class hierarchy. BINARYHEAP<E> owns an Object[] holding the complete tree in LEVEL ORDER: index 0 is the root, and for any index i the parent is at (i−1)/2 and the children at 2i+1 and 2i+2. This is the whole 'structure': there are no Node objects, no pointers, no allocation per element beyond the array — which is why heaps are so much faster in practice than their asymptotics alone suggest, since level-order layout means a parent and its children are usually in the same cache lines near the top of the array. SIZE is tracked separately from the array length, so the array can be over-allocated and grown by doubling with amortised O(1) cost per push. COMPARATOR<E> is injected, never assumed: it defines the priority order and nothing else, and injecting it is what makes min-heap and max-heap the same class. The INVARIANT — for a min-heap, every node is ≤ both of its children, and the tree is complete (all levels full except possibly the last, which is filled left to right) — is the single most important thing to state out loud, because both primitives are defined as 'restore this invariant after a local violation'. SIFTUP(i) fixes a violation at i where the element may be smaller than its parent, by repeatedly swapping upward; SIFTDOWN(i) fixes a violation where the element may be larger than a child, by repeatedly swapping with the SMALLER child. Every public operation is a composition: push is append-then-siftUp; pop is take-root, move-last-to-root, siftDown; heapify is siftDown over the internal nodes in reverse order; decreaseKey is 'change the value, then siftUp'. The optional INDEXMAP<E, Integer> is a separate collaborator that records where each element currently lives in the array, updated on EVERY swap — it exists solely to make decreaseKey and remove(e) possible, and it is the one piece that turns a heap into an indexed priority queue."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The design conversation is about the CONTRACT, not the code, and there are four decisions worth defending. First, ARRAY-BACKED COMPLETE TREE over an explicit node tree. The completeness property is what makes the array representation exact and gapless, and it is not incidental — it is enforced by always inserting at the end and always removing by moving the last element to the root, which are the only two operations that change the shape. The payoff is no pointer overhead, no allocation per element, and excellent locality; the cost is that the structure cannot be split or merged cheaply, which is precisely where leftist heaps, skew heaps, and pairing heaps earn their keep. Second, COMPARATOR INJECTION over Comparable. It decouples ordering from the element type (so the same task objects can be queued by deadline in one place and by cost in another), it makes max-heap a one-line reversed comparator, and it is the reason Java's PriorityQueue takes one. Third, EXPOSE ONLY WHAT THE INVARIANT SUPPORTS. A heap is a partial order, not a sorted list, and the API should not pretend otherwise: no get(i), no indexOf, and an iterator that documents its order as unspecified. Designs that leak the array (returning it, or letting a caller mutate elements in place) allow a caller to break the invariant silently, and the failure appears much later at an unrelated pop — so elements should be effectively immutable with respect to their priority, and any priority change must go through the queue's own decreaseKey. That single rule is the most valuable API decision in the whole design. Fourth, the INDEXED VARIANT as an explicit, separate type or an opt-in flag rather than an always-on cost: the position map roughly doubles the memory and adds a hash-map write to every swap, so making every user pay for a feature only Dijkstra needs is the wrong default. Patterns are thin here and that is correct — a heap does not need Strategy or Factory — but two are real: the Comparator IS a Strategy, and building an indexed heap by composition (a heap plus a map, with the map updated inside the swap primitive) rather than by inheritance is what keeps the swap discipline in exactly one method, which matters enormously because a single missed map update corrupts the structure in a way that is nearly impossible to debug."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "class BinaryHeap<E> {\n  private Object[] a;\n  private int size;\n  private final Comparator<? super E> cmp;              // min-heap by default; reverse for max\n  private final Map<E, Integer> pos;                     // null unless this is an INDEXED heap\n\n  BinaryHeap(int capacity, Comparator<? super E> cmp, boolean indexed) {\n    a = new Object[Math.max(1, capacity)];\n    this.cmp = cmp;\n    this.pos = indexed ? new HashMap<>() : null;\n  }\n\n  @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\") private E at(int i) { return (E) a[i]; }\n  private static int parent(int i) { return (i - 1) >>> 1; }\n  private static int left(int i)   { return 2 * i + 1; }\n\n  /** The ONE place elements move. The index map is updated here and nowhere else. */\n  private void swap(int i, int j) {\n    Object t = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = t;\n    if (pos != null) { pos.put(at(i), i); pos.put(at(j), j); }\n  }\n\n  private void siftUp(int i) {\n    while (i > 0 && cmp.compare(at(i), at(parent(i))) < 0) { swap(i, parent(i)); i = parent(i); }\n  }\n\n  private void siftDown(int i) {\n    for (;;) {\n      int l = left(i), r = l + 1, best = i;\n      if (l < size && cmp.compare(at(l), at(best)) < 0) best = l;\n      if (r < size && cmp.compare(at(r), at(best)) < 0) best = r;   // swap with the SMALLER child\n      if (best == i) return;\n      swap(i, best); i = best;\n    }\n  }\n\n  public void push(E e) {\n    if (size == a.length) a = Arrays.copyOf(a, size * 2);   // amortised O(1) growth\n    a[size] = e;\n    if (pos != null) pos.put(e, size);\n    siftUp(size++);                                          // O(log n)\n  }\n\n  public E peek() {\n    if (size == 0) throw new NoSuchElementException();\n    return at(0);                                            // O(1)\n  }\n\n  public E pop() {\n    E top = peek();\n    swap(0, --size);                                         // last element to the root\n    a[size] = null;                                          // no loitering\n    if (pos != null) pos.remove(top);\n    if (size > 0) siftDown(0);                               // O(log n)\n    return top;\n  }\n\n  /** Bottom-up build: O(n), NOT O(n log n) — see the deep dive. */\n  static <E> BinaryHeap<E> heapify(E[] items, Comparator<? super E> cmp) {\n    BinaryHeap<E> h = new BinaryHeap<>(items.length, cmp, false);\n    System.arraycopy(items, 0, h.a, 0, items.length);\n    h.size = items.length;\n    for (int i = (h.size >>> 1) - 1; i >= 0; i--) h.siftDown(i);   // leaves are already heaps\n    return h;\n  }\n\n  /** Indexed heaps only: the operation Dijkstra needs and a plain heap cannot offer. */\n  public void decreaseKey(E e) {\n    if (pos == null) throw new UnsupportedOperationException(\"not an indexed heap\");\n    Integer i = pos.get(e);\n    if (i == null) { push(e); return; }\n    siftUp(i);                                               // priority only improved\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the array representation and the two primitives everything composes from",
        "Start with why the array works at all, because the reasoning is the structure. A binary heap is required to be a COMPLETE tree: every level is full except possibly the last, which fills left to right. Completeness is exactly the condition under which level-order numbering has no gaps, so the tree can be stored as a dense array where index 0 is the root, and for any i the children are at 2i+1 and 2i+2 and the parent at (i−1)/2 (using a logical right shift, since floor division of a non-negative int is what the formula needs). Two consequences follow immediately: the tree's height is ⌊log₂ n⌋, which bounds every path-walking operation at O(log n); and the last element is always at index size−1, which is what makes both shape-changing operations trivial. Note that completeness is not something the code checks — it is preserved by construction, because the only insertion point is the end and the only removal is of the root, filled by the last element. SIFT-UP restores the invariant after a violation at a node that may be SMALLER than its parent: compare with the parent, swap if out of order, repeat. It terminates at the root or when the parent is already ≤ the node, and it touches at most one node per level, so O(log n). It is used by push (append at the end, then sift up) and by decrease-key. SIFT-DOWN restores the invariant after a violation at a node that may be LARGER than a child: compare with BOTH children, swap with the SMALLER of them (for a min-heap), and repeat from the new position. Swapping with the smaller child is the detail people get wrong — swapping with an arbitrary child can put a larger element above a smaller sibling and leave the invariant broken with no error, producing a heap that mostly works and occasionally pops the wrong element, the worst kind of bug. Sift-down is used by pop (move the last element to the root, then sift down) and by heapify and increase-key. Everything else in the API is one of these two plus bookkeeping, and saying that explicitly — 'there are two primitives; the rest is composition' — is the clearest way to present the design. Two correctness details worth volunteering: pop must null out the vacated slot so the array does not hold a reference the caller expects to be released (a real memory leak in long-lived queues); and growth by doubling gives amortised O(1) per push, with the honest note that the resize copy is an O(n) hiccup, which matters for latency-sensitive users and is one reason a fixed-capacity heap is sometimes preferred."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: why bottom-up heapify is O(n) while n pushes are O(n log n)",
        "This is the single most commonly examined complexity claim about heaps, and 'it's O(n), I remember that' is not an answer — the interviewer wants the summation. Building by repeated PUSH: element k is appended and sifted up through a tree of height ⌊log₂ k⌋, so the total is Σ log k for k = 1..n, which is log(n!) = Θ(n log n). And the cost is concentrated where the elements are: the vast majority of pushes happen when the heap is already large, so almost every push pays close to the full log n. Building BOTTOM-UP (Floyd's heapify): copy the array as-is, then run sift-down on every internal node from index n/2 − 1 down to 0. The correctness argument is a simple induction — the second half of the array is all leaves, and a single node is trivially a valid heap; processing indices in decreasing order means both children of node i are already the roots of valid heaps when siftDown(i) runs, so sift-down is exactly the operation that merges two valid heaps under a new root. Now the cost. Sift-down from a node at HEIGHT h costs O(h), and the crucial asymmetry is that in a complete tree the nodes are overwhelmingly near the BOTTOM, where h is small: roughly n/2 nodes are leaves at height 0 (cost zero), n/4 at height 1, n/8 at height 2, and so on. Total work is Σ over h of (n / 2^(h+1)) · O(h) = O(n · Σ h/2^h), and Σ h/2^h converges to 2, giving O(2n) = O(n). Contrast that with sift-UP-based building, where the asymmetry runs the wrong way: sift-up from a node at DEPTH d costs O(d), and half the nodes are at maximum depth, so the same summation gives Θ(n log n). The one-line summary worth memorising and being able to derive: sift-down is cheap for the many shallow-from-the-bottom nodes; sift-up is expensive for the many deep nodes. Two practical corollaries. First, if you are given an array and need a heap, always use heapify — the constant factor is also better because it does one pass with no array growth. Second, this is why HEAPSORT is O(n log n) and not better: the build is O(n), but the n extractions each cost O(log n), and that second phase dominates. It is also worth noting what heapify does NOT give you — it does not sort, and the resulting array is a valid heap in which only index 0 is guaranteed to be the minimum; candidates sometimes claim heapify produces a sorted array, which is a quick way to reveal the property was memorised rather than understood."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the decrease-key problem — position maps vs lazy deletion",
        "This is the design question hiding inside the data structure, and it is the reason the topic belongs in an LLD track rather than an algorithms one. Dijkstra's algorithm needs to say 'the tentative distance to node 47 just improved from 12 to 9 — reprioritise it'. A plain binary heap cannot do that, and the reason is structural, not an oversight: the heap's array is only PARTIALLY ordered, so finding an arbitrary element requires a linear scan of all n entries. An O(n) search on every relaxation destroys the algorithm's complexity, so 'just scan for it' is not an option. There are exactly two sound answers, and knowing both plus their trade is the whole point. OPTION ONE — THE POSITION MAP (an indexed priority queue). Maintain a Map<E, Integer> (or, when keys are dense integers like node ids, an int[] indexed by id, which is faster and allocation-free) recording each element's current array index. decreaseKey(e) then becomes: look up the index in O(1), overwrite the priority, and siftUp from there in O(log n). The discipline that makes this work — and the thing that makes it fragile — is that the map must be updated on EVERY movement of EVERY element, which is why the code above confines all movement to a single swap() method and updates the map there. Any other place that writes into the array, including a clever optimisation that shifts an element without swapping (the standard 'hole' optimisation that moves a hole down and places the element once), must update the map too, and forgetting one path leaves stale indices that corrupt later operations in ways that surface far from the bug. Costs: roughly double the memory for an object-keyed map, a hash write on every swap, and the requirement that elements have stable identity and correct equals/hashCode. Benefit: the heap holds at most V entries, memory is bounded, and there are no stale entries to skip. OPTION TWO — LAZY DELETION (the 'push a duplicate' trick). Never update anything: when a node's distance improves, simply PUSH a new (distance, node) pair. The heap now holds several entries for the same node, but the best one is always popped first because it has the smallest key. On pop, check whether the popped entry's distance still matches the node's current best (or keep a visited/settled set) and if not, DISCARD it and pop again. This is what nearly every production and competitive implementation does, and its virtues are real: no map, no index bookkeeping, no way to corrupt the structure, and the code is a dozen lines. Its costs are equally real: the heap can hold up to E entries instead of V, so memory grows with edges, and each push/pop is O(log E) instead of O(log V) — which is the same asymptotically for simple graphs (log E ≤ 2 log V) but a genuine constant-factor and memory difference on dense graphs. THE RULE TO STATE: use lazy deletion by default because it is simpler and cannot be corrupted; use a position map when memory is tight, when the graph is dense enough that duplicate entries matter, or when you genuinely need remove(arbitrary element) as an API operation, which lazy deletion cannot provide at all. Finally, the related API questions the interviewer may fold in: INCREASE-KEY is the mirror (siftDown instead of siftUp) and lazy deletion does NOT handle it, because a worsened priority does not surface first — that asymmetry is a good detail to volunteer. And STABILITY: a binary heap is not stable, since equal-priority elements are ordered by array position after arbitrary swaps; if FIFO among equals is required, make the comparator break ties on a monotonically increasing insertion sequence number, which is exactly how a stable priority queue is built and is a cleaner answer than trying to make the heap itself stable."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "BINARY HEAP vs OTHER HEAPS: the binary heap is the right default — O(log n) push and pop, O(1) peek, O(n) build, minimal memory, superb cache behaviour from the level-order array. A d-ARY HEAP (four children per node) reduces height to log_d n, making push and decrease-key faster at the cost of d comparisons per sift-down level; it is a genuine win for decrease-key-heavy workloads like Dijkstra on dense graphs, and it is a good answer to 'can you make it faster?' A FIBONACCI HEAP gives O(1) amortised decrease-key and improves Dijkstra's theoretical bound to O(E + V log V), but its constants and pointer-chasing make it slower than a binary heap in practice for almost all real graph sizes — knowing that the theoretically superior structure loses in practice is exactly the kind of judgement being probed. PAIRING, LEFTIST, and SKEW heaps support O(log n) or better MELD, which the array representation fundamentally cannot: merging two array heaps means rebuilding, O(n + m). If merging is a requirement, the array representation is the wrong choice, and recognising that the requirement drives the representation is the design lesson. SORTED ARRAY or BALANCED BST as alternatives: a sorted structure gives O(1) min and ordered iteration and search, but O(n) insert (array) or O(log n) with much worse constants and memory (tree) — take a heap when you only ever need the extreme, take a balanced BST when you also need rank, range queries, or ordered traversal. INDEXED vs LAZY, as above: opt-in, not default. THREAD SAFETY: this heap is not thread-safe and should not pretend to be; a lock-wrapped heap serialises everything, which is fine for moderate contention, while Java's PriorityBlockingQueue does exactly that with a lock plus a notEmpty condition — and a truly concurrent priority queue (skip-list based) is a specialised structure worth naming rather than hand-rolling. EXTENSIONS worth mentioning: a BOUNDED top-k heap that keeps the k best by using a MIN-heap of size k and discarding anything smaller than the root (a classic interview follow-up whose trick is that finding the top k largest uses a min-heap, not a max-heap); a MEDIAN structure built from two heaps facing each other; and TIMER/scheduler queues, which are the most common production use and which add the requirement of cancelling a scheduled task — that is remove(arbitrary), which sends you straight back to the position map, closing the loop between the API question and the representation."),
    ],
    "The heap looks like an algorithms warm-up and is used as a design question: anyone can recite sift-up and sift-down, so interviewers push on the contract. The graded moves are deriving the array representation from the completeness property and its index arithmetic, presenting push/pop/heapify as compositions of exactly two primitives, deriving the O(n) bottom-up build with the Σ h/2^h argument rather than asserting it, injecting a Comparator instead of hardcoding an order, and — the discriminating question — recognising that a heap offers no way to locate an arbitrary element, so decrease-key demands either a position map maintained inside a single swap primitive or the lazy-deletion pattern of pushing duplicates and skipping stale pops. Candidates who can also say when a d-ary heap wins, why a Fibonacci heap loses in practice, and why merging pushes you off the array representation entirely are demonstrating exactly the judgement the question exists to measure.",
    [
      G("heapify_complexity", "Claiming that building a heap from an array is O(n log n), or that repeated push and bottom-up heapify are equivalent.", "Derive it: sift-down from height h costs O(h) and n/2^(h+1) nodes sit at height h, so the sum is n·Σ h/2^h = O(n); sift-up-based building has the asymmetry reversed (half the nodes are at maximum depth) and is Θ(n log n). Always heapify when given an array.", "Building a heap with a loop of pushes when the whole array is available; stating O(n) without being able to produce the summation; claiming heapify sorts the array."),
      G("no_decrease_key_plan", "Designing a heap for Dijkstra with no way to reprioritise an element — or proposing a linear scan to find it.", "Choose deliberately: an indexed heap with a position map updated inside the single swap() primitive (O(1) lookup, O(log n) sift), or lazy deletion — push a duplicate entry and skip stale pops using a settled set — which is simpler and uncorruptible but holds up to E entries and cannot support remove(arbitrary).", "A scan over the array to locate an element; a position map updated in some code paths but not others (hole-based sift optimisations are the usual culprit); no answer for how a stale duplicate is recognised on pop."),
      G("invariant_and_api_leaks", "Swapping with an arbitrary child during sift-down, hardcoding the comparison order, or exposing the backing array so callers can mutate priorities in place.", "Always swap with the smaller child (min-heap); inject a Comparator so min/max is a reversed comparator; keep the array private, document iteration order as unspecified, treat priority as immutable outside decreaseKey, and null vacated slots on pop.", "A heap that mostly works but occasionally pops a non-minimum; a separate MaxHeap class duplicating the code; callers mutating an element's priority field directly; iteration assumed to be sorted; equal-priority elements assumed to come out FIFO with no sequence-number tie-break."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.heap, "Heap and priority queue class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── DISJOINT SET (UNION-FIND) ────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m17", "lld_m17_t2", 2, "Design a Disjoint Set (Union-Find)", "design-union-find",
    ["case-study", "data-structure", "algorithms", "amortized"],
    "Ten million friendship edges arrive as a stream, and after each one you must answer 'are A and B in the same component?' and 'how many components remain?'. Your first implementation attaches one root under another arbitrarily and, on adversarial input, degenerates into a ten-million-node linked list — every query becomes a walk. Then someone asks you to UNDO the last thousand unions. Design the disjoint set, and say honestly what it cannot do.",
    "Union-Find is a forest where each set is a tree and the ROOT is the set's identity, so 'same set?' reduces to 'same root?'. Two optimisations make it fast and they fix different problems: union by RANK/SIZE bounds tree HEIGHT by always attaching the smaller tree under the larger, while PATH COMPRESSION flattens the specific path a find just walked so the next query is cheap. Either alone leaves a bad case; together they give the famous inverse-Ackermann amortized bound, effectively constant. The design maturity is in what the structure REFUSES: no split, no undo (unless you give up compression and keep a rollback log), and no enumeration of a set's members without extra structure.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: maintain a partition of n elements into DISJOINT SETS under two operations. find(x) returns a canonical representative of the set containing x, so that connected(x, y) is simply find(x) == find(y). union(x, y) merges the two sets containing x and y into one, and should report whether a merge actually happened (they may already be together) — that boolean is what Kruskal's algorithm and cycle detection consume, so returning void is a real API mistake. Elements are typically 0..n−1 integers; when they are arbitrary objects, an id-mapping layer sits on top rather than inside the structure. AUGMENTATIONS that make it useful in practice and that interviewers ask for by name: componentCount() maintained incrementally (decremented on every successful union, never recomputed by scanning); size(x) or the size of x's component, which is free if you are already doing union by size; and, in more advanced use, a weighted union-find that maintains each node's OFFSET relative to its root so 'what is the difference between x and y?' can be answered when they are related. Non-functional requirements are the interesting part: both operations must be effectively constant time amortized, because the workload is a long stream of interleaved unions and queries and any per-operation logarithmic factor is multiplied by tens of millions; memory must be O(n) with a small constant — two int arrays, not objects; the structure must survive ADVERSARIAL input, meaning the order of unions must not be able to build a degenerate deep tree; and, if a recursive find is used, the recursion depth on such input must be considered explicitly. State clearly what is OUT of scope, because the boundary is the design's most interesting property: there is no split, no deletion of an element from a set, no removal of an edge, and no efficient way to LIST the members of a set — all of these require additional structure or a different data structure entirely."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "DISJOINTSET is the whole thing, and its state is two or three parallel int arrays — deliberately not objects, because n is large and the constant factor is the point. PARENT[i] holds the index of i's parent, with a root marked by parent[i] == i (the standard self-loop convention; storing −1 works too, and some implementations pack the negated SIZE into the parent slot of a root so one array does both jobs — worth mentioning as a real memory optimisation). The forest is implicit in that array: each tree is one set, and its root is the set's identity. Nothing about the tree's SHAPE carries meaning — the parent links are not the friendship edges and the tree is not the graph; it is purely a device for finding a common representative fast, which is why the structure is free to rearrange itself during a find. That point is worth stating explicitly because it is what licenses path compression, and candidates who think the tree represents the input graph cannot accept that a query mutates the structure. RANK[i] (or SIZE[i]) is the balancing metadata: rank is an upper bound on the tree's height used only for choosing which root becomes the child; size is the number of elements in the tree, which balances just as well and has the advantage of being independently useful, since size(find(x)) is then a free query. COMPONENTCOUNT is a single int, initialised to n and decremented on every successful union — an O(1) maintained aggregate rather than something recomputed. FIND is the read operation that mutates (compression); UNION composes two finds with a rank/size comparison and a single parent assignment. Optional collaborators: an IDMAPPER (Map<T, Integer>) when elements are objects, kept outside the core so the hot arrays stay primitive; a ROLLBACKLOG (a stack of the exact (childRoot, parentRoot, oldRank) records) for the undoable variant; and per-root aggregates (component size, min/max element, an XOR or sum) that can be merged in O(1) at union time — the general pattern being 'any aggregate with an associative merge can ride along on the root'."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "There is almost no class design here and that is the honest answer — the design content is the INVARIANTS and the API contract. Four decisions carry it. First, ROOT AS IDENTITY: connected(x, y) is find(x) == find(y), and the representative is whatever the structure currently finds convenient. The API must therefore never promise that a representative is STABLE — it changes when sets merge, and callers who store a root id and compare it later have a bug. Say that out loud; it is the most common misuse of the structure in real code. Second, FIND MUTATES, and the API should not hide it behind a const-looking method without comment: path compression means a read operation rewrites parent pointers, which has three consequences worth naming — a 'read' is not thread-safe without synchronisation, the amortized bound depends on it happening, and any snapshot/undo functionality is destroyed by it. Third, UNION RETURNS A BOOLEAN. Kruskal's algorithm, cycle detection in an undirected graph, and 'count the components after these edges' all consume exactly that signal, and computing it by comparing counts before and after is clumsier and slower. Fourth, BALANCING METADATA IS ON THE ROOT ONLY: rank/size values for non-root nodes are stale and meaningless, so any augmentation must be merged into the surviving root at union time and read only via find. Getting that wrong — reading size[x] instead of size[find(x)] — is the classic augmentation bug. Patterns: none of the GoF ones apply, and reaching for them would be noise; the relevant design ideas are AMORTIZED ANALYSIS as a design justification (the structure is fast on a SEQUENCE of operations, not on any single one, which matters for latency-sensitive callers), and COMPOSITION for augmentations — component size, count, and weighted offsets are separate arrays that ride along, not subclasses. On generics: keep the core primitive (int arrays) and put object mapping in a thin wrapper, because a HashMap-backed parent map is several times slower and destroys the memory argument that makes the structure attractive at scale."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "class DisjointSet {\n  private final int[] parent;\n  private final int[] size;          // union by SIZE: also gives componentSize() for free\n  private int components;\n\n  DisjointSet(int n) {\n    parent = new int[n];\n    size = new int[n];\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { parent[i] = i; size[i] = 1; }   // n singleton sets\n    components = n;\n  }\n\n  /** ITERATIVE find with full path compression — no recursion depth to blow. */\n  int find(int x) {\n    int root = x;\n    while (parent[root] != root) root = parent[root];   // pass 1: locate the root\n    while (parent[x] != root) {                          // pass 2: point everything at it\n      int next = parent[x];\n      parent[x] = root;\n      x = next;\n    }\n    return root;\n  }\n\n  /** Returns TRUE only if a merge actually happened — Kruskal and cycle detection need this. */\n  boolean union(int a, int b) {\n    int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);\n    if (ra == rb) return false;                          // already together: an edge that closes a cycle\n    if (size[ra] < size[rb]) { int t = ra; ra = rb; rb = t; }   // smaller tree under larger\n    parent[rb] = ra;\n    size[ra] += size[rb];                                // aggregate merged into the SURVIVING root\n    components--;\n    return true;\n  }\n\n  boolean connected(int a, int b) { return find(a) == find(b); }\n  int componentSize(int x) { return size[find(x)]; }     // NEVER size[x] — only roots are valid\n  int componentCount() { return components; }\n}\n\n/** Undoable variant: NO path compression, union by size only, plus a rollback log.\n *  Used by offline dynamic connectivity (divide and conquer over the time axis). */\nclass RollbackDisjointSet {\n  private final int[] parent, size;\n  private final Deque<int[]> log = new ArrayDeque<>();   // {childRoot, parentRoot, oldParentSize}\n  private int components;\n\n  RollbackDisjointSet(int n) {\n    parent = new int[n]; size = new int[n];\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { parent[i] = i; size[i] = 1; }\n    components = n;\n  }\n\n  int find(int x) { while (parent[x] != x) x = parent[x]; return x; }   // O(log n): NO compression\n\n  boolean union(int a, int b) {\n    int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);\n    if (ra == rb) { log.push(new int[]{ -1, -1, -1 }); return false; }  // record the no-op too\n    if (size[ra] < size[rb]) { int t = ra; ra = rb; rb = t; }\n    log.push(new int[]{ rb, ra, size[ra] });\n    parent[rb] = ra; size[ra] += size[rb]; components--;\n    return true;\n  }\n\n  void undo() {\n    int[] e = log.pop();\n    if (e[0] == -1) return;\n    parent[e[0]] = e[0];        // detach the child root...\n    size[e[1]] = e[2];          // ...and restore the parent's size exactly\n    components++;\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: union by rank/size and path compression — why EITHER alone is not enough",
        "The naive structure is a forest with no discipline: find walks parent pointers to a root, and union attaches one root under the other arbitrarily. Its worst case is trivially bad. Union(1,0), union(2,1), union(3,2), … always attaching the new root under the old, produces a PATH — a linked list of n nodes — and every subsequent find is O(n). Ten million elements, ten million queries, O(n) each: that is the degenerate case in the prompt, and it is not a rare adversarial construction, it is what naturally happens when edges arrive in sorted or sequential order, which is extremely common. UNION BY RANK/SIZE fixes the SHAPE. Always attach the shorter (by rank) or smaller (by size) tree under the other. The union-by-size argument is the cleanest: a node's depth increases only when its tree is the smaller one being attached, and each such event at least DOUBLES the size of the tree containing it — so a node can be pushed down at most log₂ n times, and every tree has height ≤ log₂ n. Union by rank achieves the same bound by tracking an upper bound on height rather than exact size; rank increases only when two trees of equal rank merge, so a tree of rank r contains at least 2^r nodes, giving the same logarithmic ceiling. Either variant alone gives O(log n) per operation — already the difference between usable and unusable, and worth noting that this bound holds even without compression, which is exactly why the rollback variant can drop compression and remain viable. PATH COMPRESSION fixes the COST OF REPEATED WALKS. During a find, after locating the root, point every node on the traversed path directly at the root. The next find on any of those nodes is O(1), and the cost of the walk is amortised over all the queries it accelerates. This is legitimate precisely because, as noted earlier, the tree shape carries no information: only 'which root' matters, so the structure may rearrange itself freely. Compression ALONE — arbitrary union plus compression — is better than nothing but still allows a pathological build-up before the first find flattens it, and its bound is O(log n) amortized rather than the near-constant one. TOGETHER the two give the celebrated result: a sequence of m operations on n elements costs O(m · α(n)), where α is the inverse Ackermann function — a function that is at most 4 for any n that could be represented in this universe, so the operations are constant time for every practical purpose, though NOT literally O(1), and stating that distinction correctly is a small but reliable signal. The intuition for why they compose so well: rank/size keeps trees from ever getting deep in the first place, and compression collapses whatever depth does accumulate, so neither optimisation has to handle the case the other one prevents. One implementation detail: with union by RANK plus compression, ranks become upper bounds rather than exact heights (compression shortens paths without decreasing ranks) — that is harmless, since rank is only ever used comparatively, but it is a good detail to know when someone asks why the rank is not updated after a compression."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: iterative vs recursive find, and the operations union-find genuinely cannot do",
        "RECURSION DEPTH. The elegant find is two lines: if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]); return parent[x]. It compresses the path on the way back up and reads beautifully. It also recurses to the DEPTH of the tree, and while union by rank bounds that depth at log n in the steady state, there are two situations where that is not a safe assumption. First, if the structure is used without balancing — which happens in codebases where someone 'simplified' the union — the depth can reach n and a large input produces a StackOverflowError rather than a slow answer. Second, and more subtly, a bulk INITIALISATION or an adversarial construction can build a deep chain before any find has occurred to compress it; the very first find then recurses to that full depth. On a JVM with a default stack of around 512KB, that is roughly tens of thousands of frames, which a ten-million-element structure can exceed. The iterative two-pass form in the code has no such limit: walk to the root, then walk again rewriting parents. It costs a second traversal, which is essentially free because the path is already in cache. A popular middle ground is PATH HALVING — parent[x] = parent[parent[x]] on the way up, a single pass with no second walk — which achieves the same asymptotic bound with one loop and no extra memory, and is what most competitive implementations actually use; being able to name halving and splitting as one-pass alternatives to full compression is a genuine depth signal. WHAT IT CANNOT DO, which is the more valuable half of this discussion. SPLIT: there is no way to undo a union or to separate a set. The reason is structural — path compression has rewritten parent pointers of nodes that were never part of the union you want to reverse, so the information needed to restore the previous forest has been destroyed. This is why 'remove an edge from the graph and tell me the components' is not a union-find problem: dynamic connectivity WITH deletions needs either link-cut trees / Euler tour trees (fully dynamic, O(log n) or O(log² n)) or, far more commonly in practice, the OFFLINE trick — process the whole query timeline with divide and conquer, where each edge is active over a time interval, and use a UNION-FIND WITH ROLLBACK to add edges entering a segment and undo them on exit. That rollback variant is exactly the second class in the code, and note the price it pays: it must ABANDON path compression, because compression is not invertible, so every operation becomes O(log n) instead of near-constant, held up entirely by union by size. That trade — give up compression to gain undo — is the cleanest illustration of the structure's boundaries. ENUMERATION: listing the members of a set is O(n) by scanning and grouping by find, because parent pointers go the wrong way; if you need it repeatedly, maintain an explicit member list per root and splice the smaller list into the larger at union time, which costs O(n log n) total by the same small-to-large argument. And ARBITRARY DELETION of an element requires the tombstone trick — replace the element with a fresh 'ghost' index that carries its membership — because you cannot safely remove a node that other nodes may point to."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: augmentations — component counts, sizes, and weighted union-find",
        "The structure becomes genuinely useful when it carries extra information, and the general principle is worth stating before the examples: ANY aggregate over a set that can be MERGED associatively in O(1) can ride on the root and be maintained at union time — sums, counts, minima, maxima, XORs, bounding boxes. The two constraints are that the aggregate must live only on the root (values on non-root nodes are stale by definition) and that every read must go through find. COMPONENT COUNT is the simplest: initialise to n and decrement on every union that actually merges. Because union already returns that boolean, the maintenance is one line, and the alternative — recomputing by scanning all n elements — turns an O(1) query into O(n) and is a common naive answer to 'how many islands remain?'. COMPONENT SIZE comes free with union by size: size[root] is the member count, and componentSize(x) is size[find(x)]. This is the augmentation that makes union-find the natural answer to a whole family of problems: largest component after each edge (track a running maximum, updated only at unions), 'how many people are in A's network?', and percolation. WEIGHTED (or offset) UNION-FIND is the sophisticated one and it is worth explaining because it appears in real interview problems. Maintain, alongside parent[x], a value weight[x] representing x's offset RELATIVE TO ITS PARENT — for example a difference in a system of equations x − parent(x) = w, or a parity bit for bipartiteness checking, or a ratio for the 'evaluate division' problem. find(x) must then accumulate the offsets along the path to the root and, when it compresses, rewrite each node's weight to be relative to the ROOT rather than its old parent — the compression step must adjust the weights, not just the pointers, and getting that adjustment right is the entire difficulty. union(x, y, delta) computes the two roots and their accumulated offsets and sets the weight of the attached root so the relation holds; if the two are already connected, the operation instead CHECKS consistency, which is how contradictory constraints are detected. This unlocks: detecting whether an undirected graph is bipartite online (parity weights — an edge between two same-parity nodes in the same component means an odd cycle), maintaining relative positions or differences under merges, and the classic 'are these equations consistent?' problems. Two cautions: with weights, find must be iterative-with-accumulation or carefully recursive, and rollback becomes even harder because both pointers and weights must be restored. The design lesson to close on is that all of these are COMPOSITION — extra parallel arrays merged at union time — not subclasses, which is why a single well-written DisjointSet with a clean union hook serves every variant."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "UNION BY RANK vs UNION BY SIZE: asymptotically identical, both giving log n height alone and near-constant with compression. Size wins in practice because the metadata is independently useful (componentSize is free) and the reasoning is easier to explain; rank uses slightly less state conceptually and is what the classic literature uses. Pick size unless memory is truly critical. FULL COMPRESSION vs PATH HALVING vs SPLITTING: full compression needs two passes (or recursion) and produces the flattest tree; halving and splitting flatten in a single pass with the same asymptotic bound and better constants, which is why competitive and library implementations favour halving. RECURSIVE vs ITERATIVE FIND: recursion is prettier and is a stack-overflow risk on deep or unbalanced structures — take iterative by default in production code and be able to say why. INT ARRAYS vs A MAP-BACKED GENERIC VERSION: the arrays are what make the structure fast and small; a HashMap<T, T> parent map is several times slower and allocates, so map object ids to dense integers in a thin wrapper and keep the core primitive. COMPRESSION vs UNDO: the fundamental trade — you can have near-constant operations or you can have rollback, not both. Choose rollback (union by size only, with a log of exact changes) when the problem is offline dynamic connectivity or a search that must backtrack (as in some constraint solvers); choose compression otherwise. UNION-FIND vs OTHER STRUCTURES: for connectivity with EDGE DELETIONS, union-find is the wrong tool — use link-cut trees or Euler tour trees for the online case, or divide-and-conquer over time with rollback for the offline case. For connectivity queries on a STATIC graph, a single BFS/DFS labelling pass is simpler and answers in O(1) with no structure at all — reaching for union-find on a static graph is a small over-engineering tell. THREAD SAFETY: this structure is emphatically not concurrent — find mutates, so even reads race — and a lock-free union-find exists but is a research-grade artefact; the practical answer is a lock, or sharding the problem, or batching unions. WHERE IT SHOWS UP, which is the reason to know it cold: Kruskal's MST (the boolean return IS the cycle test), cycle detection in undirected graphs, connected components and island counting in streaming or grid problems, randomised Kruskal maze generation, percolation, image segmentation via connected component labelling, equivalence-class management in type inference and compilers, and account/entity merging in identity resolution — the last being the most common production use and the one where the 'representatives are not stable' warning matters most, because callers persist ids."),
    ],
    "Union-Find is the shortest data structure with the deepest interview payload: the code is thirty lines, so the entire conversation is about WHY. Interviewers grade whether you can produce the degenerate linked-list worst case of the naive version, explain that union by rank/size bounds height by a doubling argument while path compression amortises repeated walks, and state correctly that the two together give the inverse-Ackermann bound — near-constant but not literally O(1). Beyond that they probe the API (union returning whether a merge happened, representatives being unstable, aggregates living only on roots and read through find), the iterative find and the stack-depth reason for it, and the boundaries: no split, no edge deletion, and no undo unless you sacrifice path compression for a rollback log — the exact trade that offline dynamic connectivity makes. Candidates who can also sketch weighted union-find for parity or offset constraints are showing they have used the structure, not just memorised it.",
    [
      G("missing_balancing_or_compression", "Implementing union by attaching one root under the other arbitrarily, or applying only one of the two optimisations and claiming near-constant time.", "Apply BOTH: union by size/rank so a node's depth grows only when its tree is the smaller one (bounding height at log n by a doubling argument), and path compression so a find flattens the path it walked; only together do they give O(α(n)) amortized.", "parent[find(a)] = find(b) with no size or rank comparison; sequential unions producing an n-deep chain; a claim of O(1) or O(α(n)) from an implementation with compression but no balancing."),
      G("stale_root_metadata", "Reading size[x], rank[x], or an augmented aggregate directly on a node instead of on its root — or recomputing the component count by scanning.", "Keep every aggregate on the ROOT only, merge it into the surviving root inside union, and read it as agg[find(x)]; maintain componentCount as an int decremented on each successful union rather than recomputed.", "componentSize(x) returning size[x]; an aggregate updated on both roots or on the child; counting components by iterating all n elements; callers persisting a representative id and comparing it after later unions."),
      G("undo_and_deletion_assumptions", "Assuming union-find can undo a union, split a set, delete an edge, or enumerate a set's members cheaply.", "State the boundary: path compression is not invertible, so rollback requires abandoning compression and logging each union's exact (childRoot, parentRoot, oldSize) — O(log n) per op, which is what offline dynamic connectivity uses; edge deletion online needs link-cut/Euler tour trees; member enumeration needs explicit per-root lists spliced small-to-large.", "An undo() implemented on a compressing structure; using union-find for a graph where edges are removed; scanning all elements with find() to list one set on every query; recursive find risking StackOverflowError on a deep or unbalanced structure."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.unionfind, "Disjoint set (union-find) class diagram"),
];

const xpFor = (l) => (l === "warmup" ? 5 : l === "easy" ? 10 : l === "medium" ? 15 : 20);
const diffFor = (l) => (l === "warmup" ? 0.1 : l === "easy" ? 0.2 : l === "medium" ? 0.4 : 0.6);
function pm(moduleId, o) {
  return { trackKey: TRACK_KEY, moduleId, topicId: o.topicId, exerciseId: o.exerciseId,
    position: o.position, level: o.level, type: "pattern_match", title: o.title, scenario: o.scenario,
    instructions: "Pick the best answer.", starterCode: "", expectedSolution: o.correct,
    blanks: [{ options: o.options }], testCases: [{ type: "pattern_match", correct: o.correct, explanation: o.explanation }],
    hints: [], xpReward: xpFor(o.level), difficulty: diffFor(o.level) };
}

